\begin{abstract}
Speech is a noninvasive digital phenotype that can offer valuable insights into mental health conditions, but it is often treated as a single modality. In contrast, we propose the treatment of patient speech data as a trimodal multimedia data source for depression detection. This study explores the potential of large language model-based architectures for speech-based depression prediction in a multimodal regime that integrates speech-derived text, acoustic landmarks, and vocal biomarkers. Adolescent depression presents a significant challenge and is often comorbid with multiple disorders, such as suicidal ideation and sleep disturbances. This presents an additional opportunity to integrate multi-task learning (MTL) into our study by simultaneously predicting depression, suicidal ideation, and sleep disturbances using the multimodal formulation. We also propose a longitudinal analysis strategy that models temporal changes across multiple clinical interactions, allowing for a comprehensive understanding of the conditions' progression. Our proposed approach, featuring trimodal, longitudinal MTL is evaluated on the Depression Early Warning dataset. It achieves a balanced accuracy of 70.8\%, which is higher than each of the unimodal, single-task, and non-longitudinal methods.

% Speech is noninvasive and multimodal data source  contains both linguistic and acoustic features, offering valuable insights into these mental health conditions. Adolescent depression presents a significant challenge, and it is often comorbid with multiple disorders such as suicidal ideation and sleep disturbances.  This study explores the potential of large language model (LLM)-based architectures for speech-based depression prediction using a multimodal approach that integrates text, acoustic landmarks, and vocal biomarkers. We propose a novel multi-task learning framework that simultaneously predicts depression, suicidal ideation, and sleep disturbances using perceptual raw speech data and text. We also introduce a longitudinal analysis strategy that models temporal changes across multiple clinical interactions, allowing for a more comprehensive understanding of the conditions' progression. Our proposed approach is evaluated on the Depression Early Warning dataset using the Mental-Alpaca and LLaMA-2-7B models, demonstrating an ability to leverage multimodal speech features for improved prediction of multiple mental health tasks. Our results demonstrate the improved performance of our proposed multimodal longitudinal pipeline compared to the baseline unimodal approach.
\end{abstract}

