\subsection{MASS-BRPE}

The goal of MASS-BRPE is to estimate key room parameters from reverberant speech $\bm{\mathbf{s}}_{\mathrm{r}}(n)$ and to obtain room acoustic features related to these parameters, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{brpe}. These room parameters include the room volume $\mathcal{V}$, \rts, and a dynamic boundary point $\mathcal{B}_\mathrm{p}$ between early and late reverberation. Compared to traditional in-situ measurement methods, blind estimation is more convenient for labeling, as it does not rely on intrusive measurements or expert knowledge. 
% Through this method, we can accurately estimate the RIR that matches the actual room environment using only reverberant audio signals.

To enhance efficiency by reducing GPU memory consumption and computational inference time, we adopt the latest SSMs, particularly those based on the Mamba framework \cite{gu2023mamba, shams2024ssamba,wu2025cross}. 
% These methods achieve performance comparable to Transformer-based methods while avoiding their complexity, offering a more efficient alternative.
Inspired by the work in \cite{gu2023mamba, shams2024ssamba,wang2025ss}, we propose the MASS-BRPE module. During the pre-training phase, input spectrograms are divided into non-overlapping patches, with a subset randomly masked. Embeddings of these masked patches are then used as training targets, focusing on both discriminative and generative tasks. 

In the fine-tuning and inference stages, the input and output dimensions of the model are adjusted to accommodate the MASS-BRPE module. Specifically, spectral features, phase features, and the first-order derivatives of phase coefficients are extracted along the frequency axis to serve as the module’s inputs. By applying the Gammatone ERB filter, we aggregate these features along the feature dimensions to construct a 2-D feature block. This feature configuration is consistent with the ``+\textit{Phase}" strategy and has been proven to outperform methods based solely on amplitude spectral features in terms of performance \cite{ick2023blind, wang2025ss}.
% which estimates key room parameters in real-world environments from a single-channel speech signal and extracts room acoustic features intrinsically linked to these parameters.

% In the MASS-BRPE module, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{brpe}. The 2-D feature blocks extracted from the audio are subdivided into $J$ patches of $16 \times 16$ each. Each patch $S_{[j]}$ is first transformed through a linear projection layer, termed the patch embedding layer, into a 1-D patch embedding of length 768, denoted $E_{[j]}$. To capture temporal and spatial information within each patch, learnable positional embeddings  $P_{[j]}$ are introduced and added to $E_{[j]}$ to form the combined embeddings ($E_{[j]}+P_{[j]}$). These combined embeddings are then processed through a Mamba encoder with a bidirectional SSM, producing the encoder output $O_{[j]}$, which represents an advanced representation of the spectrogram patches.

