Despite introducing the additional magnitude vector, DoRA maintains a significantly lower computational overhead compared to full-parameter fine-tuning. The rank \( r \) is typically much smaller than the dimensions \( d \) and \( k \) (usually $r = 4, 8$), making the number of parameters in \( A \) and \( B \) substantially smaller than in the original weight matrix \( W \), thus reducing the training burden. 
\begin{equation}
\gamma = \frac{\text{new parameters}}{\text{original parameters}} = \frac{d*r + r*k + k}{{d*k}} \label{eq7}
\end{equation}
