\section{Pre-training with MXFP8}
\label{sec:training}

Model weights, activations, and gradient tensors are quantized from  \texttt{FP32}  into \texttt{MXFP8} during the forward and backward passes in training. \texttt{MXFP8} tensors are then stored and operated on by the hardware. We first describe the conversion process, \verb+Quantize_to_fp8+$(V_i / 2^X)$, starting with scale computation and following on to the quantization procedure for each element. The quantization process described below is the same across all \texttt{MX}-formats (\texttt{E4M3}, \texttt{E5M2} , \texttt{E2M3}, \texttt{E3M2}, and \texttt{E2M1}), with only the \texttt{MX} data type differing.

\subsection{Conversion from FP32 to MXFP8}
\label{sec:conversion}

\textbf{Computing X:} Typically, most of the values within each block in a tensor will be out of the representable range for the target \texttt{MX} format, both underflowing the minimum  and overflowing the maximum representable number. To address this, a scale factor is multiplied with all values within a block, to shift the maximum number of values to be within the representable range.

The scale factor, \verb+X+, is computed based on the absolute maximum value (\verb+amax+) among the 32 high-precision input values, i.e. \verb+amax+ = \(\max\|V_{i}\|; 1 \leq i \leq 32\). The goal is to map this \verb+amax+ in the input-format to be the largest representable value in the desired \texttt{MX}-format. Special care is taken if some or all the elements in the input are \verb+Infinity+ and/or not-a-number (\verb+NaN+). If \verb+amax+ in a block is 0, then \verb+X+ is set to \verb+-127+, thus, interpreted value of \verb+X+ is $2^{-127}$. All $Q_i$'s in this case are set to 0. 

When \verb+X+ is not \verb+Inf+, \verb+NaN+ or 0, the OCP~\cite{ocp} specification suggests \verb+X+ be set to the largest power-of-two less than or equal to \verb+amax+ divided by the largest power-of-two representable in the \texttt{MX}-format type. For example, with the \texttt{E4M3} type, $\verb+X+ = \frac{\text{floor}(\log_2(\texttt{amax}))}{\text{floor}(\log_2(448))}$, since 448 is the largest magnitude in \texttt{E4M3}. Thus, the OCP specification ignores the floating-point significand of this ratio.\footnote{Assume \texttt{amax} and max representable in \texttt{MX}-format (\texttt{destamax}) are \textit{normal} floating-point numbers of the form \texttt{amax} = $2^A$ × 1.$M_a$ and \texttt{destmax} = $2^E$ × 1.$M_e$ (where mantissa $M_a$ and $M_e$ lie between 0 (inclusive) and 1. OCP rounding scheme results in scale exponent = A-E (ignoring the case where min(A-E) is clamped to -127)}

We observe accuracy degradation when following the OCP specification. % for computing the scale factor. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:ocp_vs_ours} show training loss curves for a 843 million parameter transformer model trained under two token horizons: 300 billion and 1 trillion tokens. Two different configurations are studied: 

\begin{itemize}[label={--}]

\item  (\verb+cfg+1) \texttt{E4M3} for all tensors (weights (W), activations (A) and gradients (G)), and %\\

\item (\verb+cfg+2) \texttt{E4M3} for W and A tensors, \texttt{E5M2} for G tensors. 

\end{itemize}

The \texttt{E5M2} format has $\sim$1.8x more binades than \texttt{E4M3} and since gradients typically have larger dynamic range, \cite{micikevicius2022fp8formatsdeeplearning} advocated the use of \texttt{E5M2} with per-tensor scaling method. This rationale underpins the study of \texttt{E5M2} gradients. In both \verb+cfg+1 and \verb+cfg+2, scale factor computation using OCP method leads to training divergence (Fig.~\ref{fig:843m_300b}) or a widening loss gap relative to \texttt{BF16} (Fig.~\ref{fig:843m_1t}).


