%\newpage
Algorithm~\ref{alg:scale} outlines our method for computing the scale factor. The key change is to \textit{round-up towards positive infinity} the exponent of ratio of \verb+amax+  and max representable in \texttt{MX}-format, \verb+destmax+ (while saturating to \verb+UE8M0+ max/min limits). 
This is in contrast to the OCP scheme which effectively suggests to \textit{round-down} the scale value. 
Since a high-precision value, $V_i$, is scaled by the scale factor $2^X$, rounding-up the denominator of the fraction ($V_i / 2^X$) has a tendency to map \verb+amax+ \textit{below} \verb+destmax+. In contrast, the OCP method has a tendency to map \verb+amax+ above \verb+destmax+ (which subsequently needs to be clamped so that it becomes representable). We hypothesize that this saturating effect with OCP rounding method affects model accuracy. A more detailed and accurate workflow to compute and round the value of \verb+X+ is described in Appendix Sec.~\ref{App_e8m0_rounding}. 

\begin{algorithm}[thb]
\caption{Our proposal for computing scale factor, X (simplified method)}\label{alg:scale}
\begin{algorithmic}
\State \(X_{\text{float}} \gets \) \verb+amax+/\verb+destmax+  \Comment{\texttt{destmax} is max representable in \texttt{MX}-format}
\State \(\text{expX}_{\text{float}} \gets \log_2(X_{\text{float}})\) \Comment{extract the exponent of X (de-biased form)}
\State \(\text{expX}_{\text{int}} \gets \text{ceil}(\text{expX}_{\text{float}}) \) \Comment{\textbf{round-up}}
\State \(X \gets \text{clamp}(\text{expX}_{\text{int}}, -127, 127) \) \Comment{clamp to min/max \texttt{E8M0} representable}
\State \(X \gets X + 127 \) \Comment{add bias}
\State return \(X\) \Comment{store X}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


Fig.~\ref{fig:ocp_vs_ours} shows that \texttt{MXFP8} with \texttt{E4M3} for gradients (blue curve) and \texttt{MXFP8} with \texttt{E5M2} for gradients (purple curve) with our proposed rounding scheme both overlap with a reference \texttt{BF16} loss curve across both 300B and 1T token horizons.
Later, in section~\ref{sec:dtype_tensors} we discuss that \texttt{E4M3} is in fact a better choice for pre-training LLMs versus \texttt{E5M2} with MX style fine-grained scaling.

\noindent\textbf{Quantizing FP32 values to MX type:} Once \verb+X+ is computed, $V_i$ is scaled by $2^X$ and the resulting value is quantized to a \texttt{FP8}-representable number. This is the \verb+Quantize_to_fp8()+ function. Round-to-nearest-ties-to-even (RN) rounding is used during this quantization step. The conversion process is saturating, i.e. if after rounding the resulting value exceeds \texttt{FP8} max or is less than \texttt{FP8} min value, then the result is clamped to respective max or min value.

A practical instance of this conversion process in low-precision LLM pre-training arises when matrix multiplication and accumulation (MMA) outputs, typically stored in \texttt{FP32}, must be mapped to \texttt{MXFP8}. In this case 8-bit quantized values are stored in memory, thus saving write bandwidth and storage capacity when compared with storing \texttt{FP32}  values. Subsequent model operation then reads \texttt{MXFP8} values, saving read bandwidth compared to loading  \texttt{FP32}  values. Further, since Tensor Cores can process \texttt{MX}-formatted inputs, the MMA operation in lower precision consumes less energy and operates at higher throughput. 


