\texttt{FP8} has two variants in Blackwell~\ref{tab:mxformat}: \texttt{E4M3} and \texttt{E5M2}. Our experiments show the following: 

\begin{itemize}[label={--}]

\item Better convergence with \texttt{E4M3} instead of \texttt{E5M2} for weights and activations during training. Fig.~\ref{fig:843m_1t_shmoo} shows the loss behavior comparing \texttt{E4M3} and \texttt{E5M2} for the same 843M model used in Fig.~\ref{fig:843m_1t}. The purple curve has \texttt{E5M2} for activations and the blue curve has \texttt{E5M2} for weights while all other tensors are in \texttt{E4M3}. Both these curves show worse loss convergence compared to using \texttt{E4M3} for all tensors (orange curve) or using \texttt{E5M2} for gradients (yellow curve).

\item \texttt{E4M3} for gradients maintains training loss parity with \texttt{BF16} pre-training, especially for models with 2 billion or more parameters. Fig.~\ref{fig:8b_1t} shows the loss behavior comparing \texttt{E4M3} and \texttt{E5M2} for the gradient tensor on an 8 billion parameter LLM trained on 1 trillion tokens: using \texttt{E4M3} gradients (orange) has lower loss than using \texttt{E5M2} gradients (yellow). 
This gap increases as the model is trained on more tokens.
%While the loss behavior was very similar between \texttt{E4M3} gradients and \texttt{E5M2}  gradients with the 843M model, we start to see worse behavior as the model capacity scales. 
This change in behavior with increasing model parameter counts underscores the importance of examining the numerical properties of formats across a broad range of model sizes.

\end{itemize}

Previously, \textit{per-tensor} scaled \texttt{FP8} studies in~\cite{nvidia2025nemotronhfamilyaccurateefficient, micikevicius2022fp8formatsdeeplearning, noune20228bitnumericalformatsdeep} and coarse-grained block-scaled \texttt{FP8} studies in~\cite{deepseekai2025deepseekv3technicalreport} used \texttt{E5M2}  for gradient tensors in lieu of \texttt{E4M3}. With fine-grained scaling, the dynamic range requirements at a 32-element block size is sufficiently captured by 17.8 binades in \texttt{E4M3} type. 
Once the range requirements are met, precision (or sampling) becomes important, and \texttt{E4M3} having 8 samples per binade is better than \texttt{E5M2} having only 4. Thus, for our \texttt{MXFP8} pre-training recipe, we quantize all three tensor types -- weights, activations, and gradients -- with the \texttt{E4M3} data type. 


