\section{Introduction}
V\'agv\"olgyi \cite{tcs/Vagvolgyi03b}
showed that it is decidable for any given 
 ground term equation systems  (GTESs for short) $E$ and $F$   whether
there is a GTES $H$ such that 
$\tthue H=\tthue E\cap \tthue F$. If the answer is yes, then we can construct such a  GTES  $H$. 
We show the dual to the above result, first we
show that 
for any GTESs $E$ and $F$, $\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
 is a congruence on the ground term algebra
if and only if 
 there exists  a GTES $H$ such that $\tthue H=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
  if and only if $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$.
  Second, we show that it is decidable  in  $O(n^2)$ 
 time for any given 
  GTESs  $E$ and $F$ whether $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$, where 
  $n$ is   the number of occurrences of symbols in $E$ plus the number of occurrences of symbols in $ F$.


For each  signature $\Sigma$ without  constants,  the set of ground terms over $\Sigma$ is the empty set, each GTES over $\Sigma $ is the empty set, and our results above are obvious. Thus, throughout the paper we consider signatures with  constants. 
We now sketch the proof of our main result.
Let 
$E$ and $F$ be GTESs over $\Sigma$, and let $W$ denote the set of subterms appearing in the GTESs $E$ and $F$.
F\"ul\"op and V\'agv\"olgyi \cite{eatcs/FuloopV91}
presented a
fast ground completion algorithm which, given GTESs  $E$ and $F$  over a  signature   $\S$,
produces in $O(n \,\mathrm{log}\; n)$ time a finite set $C\langle E, F\rangle$ of  new  constants and 
a reduced ground term rewrite system (GTRS for short) $ R \langle E, F \rangle  $
over $\S$ extended with the new constants
such  that $\tthue  E = \tthue {R\langle E, F \rangle }  \cap \ts \times \ts $.
 Here $n$ stands for the number of occurrences of symbols in $E$ plus number of occurrences of symbols in  $F$.
Furthermore, the  set $C\langle E, F\rangle$ of new constants is the set of 
the  equivalence classes of
the restriction   $\Theta\langle E, F \rangle  $
of $\tthue E  $ to the set $W$. For this purpose we proceed as follows:

In the first step  in $O(n)$
time, we create a subterm dag $G$ for $E$ and $F$  \cite{jsc/Snyder93}. We consider the vertices in the  subterm dag  $G$   as terms in $W$.
 The relation $\tau \langle E, F \rangle $ on the vertices of $G$  corresponds to $E$. We construct the  relation $\tau \langle E, F \rangle $.
 Then 
we run on $G$  a  congruence closure algorithm for the relation $\tau \langle E, F \rangle $ in $O(n\,\mathrm{log}\, n)$ time  \cite{jacm/DowneyST80}. In this way, we obtain   on
$G$ the congruence closure $\rho \langle E, F \rangle $ of the relation $\tau \langle E, F \rangle $. Considering the vertices in the  subterm dag  $G$   as terms, 
$\rho \langle E, F \rangle $ yields 
       the restriction $\Theta\langle E, F \rangle$ of $\tthue E $ to $W$. Then using $\rho \langle E, F \rangle $ and 
     $\Theta\langle E, F \rangle$,  we 
produce the GTRS $ R \langle E, F \rangle $ in $O(n)$  time. 
GTRS $ R \langle E, F \rangle $ is the set of all 
rewrite rules 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \dot{\rightarrow} a $, where 
 $\s\in \S_m$, $m\in \Nat$, and   $ a_1, \ldots, a_m, a \in C\langle E, F\rangle$, and $\{\, \s(t_1, \ldots, t_m) \mid t_1\in a_1, \ldots, t_m\in a_m\, \}\subseteq a$.
 F\"ul\"op and V\'agv\"olgyi \cite{eatcs/FuloopV91} showed that GTRS $ R \langle E, F \rangle $ is reduced, and hence is convergent.
 We say that GTRS $R \langle E, F\rangle $
is  total if for any  $\s\in \S_m$ with $m\in \Nat$, and   $ a_1, \ldots, a_m \in C\langle E, F\rangle$,  there exists in  $R \langle E, F\rangle $
a rewrite rule  with left-hand side 
$\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$.
Let $\delta$ be a  $1$-context over $\S$ which   is different from the hole symbol, and  let $c\in \S_0\cup C\langle E, F\rangle$ be a constant. 
Then we say that the ground term  $\delta[c]$ is   a proper extension  of the constant $c$. 
We say that $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ reach a constant  $a\in C\langle E, F\rangle$ from   a ground term $t$  over the ranked alphabet 
 $\S \cup C\langle E, F \rangle$   if 
 $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ reduces $t$ to $a$. 
  Furthermore, we say that GTRS $R\langle E, F \rangle$  keeps up with the  GTRS 
$R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ writing a constant  $a\in C\langle E\cup F, E\rangle$ if the following condition  holds:
for every rewrite rule  of the form 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \dot{\rightarrow} a\in  R\langle {E \cup F},\emptyset\rangle$, 
 for every  $b_1, \ldots, b_m \in C\langle E, F\rangle$ with $b_i \subseteq  a_i$
 for all $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$,  
 there exists a rewrite rule in $ R\langle E, F\rangle$ of the form 
 $\s(b_1, \ldots, b_m ) \dot{\rightarrow}  b $ for some $b\in C\langle E, F\rangle$. Note that here  $b\subseteq a$. 








We abbreviate 'directed pseudograph without parallel arcs' to 'dpwpa'.
 We define the auxiliary dpwpa   $ AUX[ E;  F] =(C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle, A[E; F])$
for the GTESs $E$ and $F$. 
Here  $C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$ is  the set of vertices, and  $A[E; F]$ is the set of directed arcs.  Every directed arc $(a, b)$ is associated with some rewrite rule
 $ \s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \dot{\rightarrow} a $ in $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$, where $b=a_i$ for some $i \in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$. Consequently, there is a  path of positive length or a cycle    in 
$AUX[ E;  F]  $ from  a vertex $a$ to  a vertex $b$ if and only if 
 $ R \langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ reach the constant
  $a$  from    a proper extension    of the constant  $b$. 
  A Boolean attribute called $keeps_E$ (respectively  $keeps_F$) of a vertex (i.e. a constant in $C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$) $a$  tells us  whether 
  $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ 
  (resp. $ R \langle F, E \rangle$) keeps up with $ R \langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ 
  writing $a$.   


We compute the following sets and relations  and dpwpa  in $O(n \, \mathrm{log} \, n )$ time: 
\begin{itemize}
\item the relations  $\rho\langle E, F\rangle$, $\rho\langle F, E\rangle$,  and $\rho\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item the  set of nullary symbols  $C\langle E, F\rangle$, $C\langle F, E\rangle$, and
$C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item the GTRS $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$  stored in a red-black tree;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ stored in a red-black tree;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle  F, E\rangle$ stored in a red-black tree; 
 \item 
the auxiliary dpwpa $ AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle,A[E; F])$ for the GTESs $E$ and $F$, and 
   for every vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup  F, \emptyset  \rangle$,
the values of  the  attributes $a.equal_E$, $a.equal_F$, $a.keeps_E$, and $a.keeps_F$.
\end{itemize} 
 Then we decide in $O(n)$ time
whether $R \langle E, F \rangle $ and  $ R \langle  F, E \rangle $    are total.
Accordingly,  we distinguish the following four  main cases.  


Main Case 1: $\Sigma$ is a unary  signature. 
     In this main case
  $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
   if and only if 
    for each $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$,  Condition (i)  or Condition (ii)  hold:

   \begin{itemize} 
 \item[(i)]     \begin{itemize} 
 \item[(a)]     $ a\in C\langle  E, F \rangle $  and 
   \item[(b)]       for each $b \in C\langle  E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle $ if there is a  path of positive length or a cycle     from  
$a$ to $b$ 
 in the auxiliary   dpwpa   $ AUX[ E;  F] \rangle=
(C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle, A[E; F])$, then  
$ R \langle E, F \rangle$  keeps up with  $ R \langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$  writing $b $.
\end{itemize}
 \item[(ii)] \begin{itemize}  \item[(a)]     $ a\in C\langle  F, E \rangle $  and 
   \item[(b)]       for each $b \in C\langle  E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle $ if there is a  path of positive length or a cycle     from  
 $a$ to $b$
 in the auxiliary   dpwpa   $ AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle,A[E; F])$, then  
$ R \langle F, E \rangle$  keeps up with  $ R \langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$  writing $b $. 
   \end{itemize}
 \end{itemize}
 We decide the above formulas by 
  carrying out a nested partial depth first search 
on the auxiliary dpwpa   
$AUX[ E;  F]  $. In the outer loop we carry out a  partial depth first search 
on $AUX[ E;  F]  $ and decide Conditions (i).(a) and (ii).(a). 
In the inner loop we visit those vertices which are positive-step reachable from the current vertex of the outer loop, and examine at each visited  vertex $a$ the value of the 
Boolean attribute  $keeps_E$ (respectively   $keeps_F$),  which tells us  whether 
  $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ (resp. $ R \langle F, E\rangle$)
   keeps up with $ R \langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ 
  writing $a$.   
In this way we decide Condition (ii).(a) (resp. Condition (ii).(b)).
      In such manner, we decide in additional
$O(n^2)$ time whether
 $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$.


Main Case 2:  Both GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ and GTRS $R\langle F, E\rangle$ are  total. 
Then  $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
if and only if each $\Theta\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ equivalence class is equal to a 
 $\Theta\langle E, F \rangle$ equivalence class or a $\Theta\langle F, E \rangle$  equivalence class.
We decide in  additional $O(n)$ time whether
 $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$.

 Main Case 3:
  $\S_k\neq \emptyset$ for some $k\geq 2$, and  at least  one of $R\langle E, F\rangle$ 
and   $R\langle F, E\rangle$ is  total.  Without loss of generality we may assume  that $ R \langle E, F \rangle $   is  total.
                Then
 $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
 if and only if 
  for each $a\in C\langle  E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle $, 
   \begin{itemize}
   \item[(1)]
     $ a\in C\langle  E, F \rangle $ or  
       $ a\in C\langle  F, E \rangle $  and 
      \item [(2)] if 
     $ a\not \in C\langle  E, F \rangle $, then  
  for each $b \in C\langle  E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle $ if there is a  path of positive length or a cycle     from  
  $a$ to $b$
 in the auxiliary dpwpa   
$AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle,A[E; F])$, then  $ R \langle F, E \rangle$  keeps up with  $ R \langle E \cup  F, \emptyset  \rangle$  writing $b $.
\end{itemize}
 In $O(n)$ time, we decide set memberships $ a\in C\langle  E, F \rangle $ and  $ a\in C\langle  F, E \rangle $
  for all  $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$.  
  Therefore we decide in $O(n)$ time whether  Condition (1) holds for all $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$. 
 We decide  in   $O(n)$ time whether   Condition (2)  holds for all $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ by carrying out a partial depth first search 
on the auxiliary dpwpa   
$ AUX[ E;  F] $ by narrowing down the set of  vertices from   which we start a session of the  search. We start the   search sessions from  those 
$C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ vertices $a$ which are  not in $C\langle E,F  \rangle$ 
 and have not been visited previously.
 If we start a search session from a vertex $a$ and there exists    a cycle leading from the vertex $a$ to itself,    then along this cycle  Algorithm PDFS visits vertex $a$ for  a second time. 
   During the current session of the search we find a  vertex $b$ with false  value for the  attribute 
 $keeps_F$  if and only if  Condition (2) does not hold for the vertex $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ from which we started the current session of the search.
   We decide  whether Condition (1) holds for all $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ 
 and whether Conditions (2) holds for all $a \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$  
 in $O(n)$ time,  in this way, we decide in  additional 
$O(n)$ time whether
 $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$.





Main Case 4:  $\S$ has a symbol of arity at least $2$,
  and GTRS  $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ and GTRS $ R \langle F, E \rangle$  are not total.   
  Then $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$
 if and only if   $E \subseteq \tthue F$ or
 $ F \subseteq \tthue E $  if and only if   $E \subseteq \Theta\langle F, E \rangle$ or 
 $ F \subseteq \Theta\langle E, F \rangle $. In this case, by
 direct inspection of the representations $\tau\langle E, F\rangle$
 of $E$,  $\tau\langle F, E\rangle$ of $F$, $\rho\langle E, F \rangle$ of  ${\Theta\langle E, F \rangle}$  and   $\rho\langle F, E \rangle$ of  
  ${\Theta\langle F, E \rangle}$ in the subterm dag  for $E$ and $F$,   
 we decide in  additional  $O(n)$ time whether
 $\tthue {E\cup F}=\tthue E\cup \tthue F$.



  Summing up the previous thoughts, our  decision algorithm goes as follows: 
  We compute the following sets and relations  and dpwpa  in $O(n \, \mathrm{log} \, n )$ time: 
\begin{itemize}
\item the relations  $\rho\langle E, F\rangle$, $\rho\langle F, E\rangle$,  and $\rho\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item the  set of nullary symbols  $C\langle E, F\rangle$, $C\langle F, E\rangle$, and
$C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item the GTRS $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$  stored in a red-black tree;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ stored in a red-black tree;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle  F, E\rangle$ stored in a red-black tree; 
 \item 
the auxiliary dpwpa $ AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle,A[E; F])$ for the GTESs $E$ and $F$, and 
   for every vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup  F, \emptyset  \rangle$,
the values of  the  attributes $a.equal_E$, $a.equal_F$, $a.keeps_E$, and $a.keeps_F$.
\end{itemize} 
 We decide whether GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ and GTRS   $R\langle F, E\rangle$ are total. 
\begin{itemize}
\item If both of them are total, then we  proceed as in main Case 2.  
\item Otherwise,
\begin{itemize}
\item  if $\S$ is a unary alphabet, then we procced as in Main Case 1.
\item If  $\S_k\neq \emptyset$ for some $k\geq 2$, and  at least  one of $R\langle E, F\rangle$ 
and   $R\langle F, E\rangle$ is  total, the we proceed as in  Main Case 3.
\item If $\S$ has a symbol of arity at least $2$,
  and GTRS  $ R \langle E, F \rangle$ and GTRS $ R \langle F, E \rangle$  are not total,  then we proceed as in  Main Case 4.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
 In Main Case 1, the  overall complexity of our  computation is   $O(n^2)$.
 In each of Main Cases 2, 3, and 4,  the  overall complexity of our  computation is   $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$.
  Main  Case 2 overlaps with Main Case 1 and Main Case 3, and is a subcase of the union of 
 Main Case 1 and Main Case 3.  

 We now describe an  alternative approach for our decision problem.
 We can think of the  GTRSs $ R \langle E, F \rangle$, $ R \langle F, E \rangle$, and $ R \langle E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ 
  as the sets of rewrite rules of bottom-up tree automata. Thence we can also apply the decision algorithm of   Champav{\`{e}}re  et al \cite{franciak}. Their 
algorithm decides  inclusion between two tree languages recognized by given bottom-up 
  tree automata. Applying their algorithm  to our decision problem in  each of Main Cases 1, 2, and 3  yields an alternative decision  algorithm, which
    is more sophisticated than ours, and in general takes more time than ours,  it runs in  $O(n^3)$ time.  

   For any given 
  GTESs  $E$ and $F$, we can construct the  GTES $E \cup F$ in  $O(n \, \mathrm{log} \, n)$ time, where 
  $n$ is  as above. 

