       \begin{sta}\label{drrszam} Let  $n=size(E)+size(F)$. Then 
       \begin{quote}
 1. $|R\langle E, F   \rangle|\leq |W|\leq size(W) \leq size (E \cup F) \leq size(E) + size(F)= n $. \newline
 2. $size(R\langle E,  F \rangle)\leq size(W)+ |W|\leq 2\cdot n $\, and \, \newline 
3. $|BSTEP\langle E, F   \rangle|\leq size(R\langle E,  F \rangle)\leq 2 \cdot n$.
\end{quote}
\end{sta} 
\begin{proof}
By direct inspection of  Definition \ref{szabaly} and by Statement \ref{meret}  we have Statement 1. 

We now show Statement 2. By Definition \ref{szabaly}, 
for each rule  $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \dot{\rightarrow} a\in R\langle E, F \rangle$, there exists a ground term $\s(t_1, \ldots, t_m)\in W$
such that $a_i=t_i/_ {\Theta\langle E, F \rangle}$ for every $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$.
Then we assign to the occurrence of $a_i$ the root symbol of the ground term $t_i$  for every $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$. Consequently $size(R\langle E,  F \rangle)\leq size(W)+ |W|$. By Statement 1, $size(W)+ |W|\leq 2\cdot n $.

By direct inspection of   Definition \ref{zug} and by Statement 2  we have Statement 3. 



\end{proof}
We now adopt the fast ground completion  algorithm of 
 F\"ul\"op and V\'agv\"olgyi \cite{eatcs/FuloopV91}
  that produces the GTRS $R\langle E, F  \rangle$.
 \begin{df}\label{alma}\rm 
Algorithm Fast Ground Completion Introducing Additional Constants (FGC for short) \cite{eatcs/FuloopV91}
\newline
Input: A  signature   $\S$,  GTES $E$ and GTES $F$ over $\Sigma$.
 \newline
Output:\begin{itemize}
\item 
 the relation $\rho\langle E, F \rangle$, 
 \item the  set of nullary symbols $C\langle E, F \rangle$, 
 \item 
 the GTRS $R\langle E, F \rangle$ stored without duplicates  in a  red-black tree $RBT1$,  and 
 \item the relation 
 $BSTEP\langle E, F  \rangle$ stored  without duplicates  in a  red-black tree $RBT2$.
  \end{itemize}
Data structures: 
The congruence closure algorithm appearing in Proposition 
\ref{lezaras},  gives each  $\rho$-class a unique name, which is an element of the set $\{\, 1, \ldots, |W|\, \}$.
We assign to each symbol of $\Sigma$ a unique  number in the set
\begin{quote}
 $\{\, |W|+1, \ldots, 2\cdot |W|\, \}$. 
  \end{quote} 
Let
\begin{quote}
$\ell= \lceil\mathrm{log} _2|W|\rceil+1$. 
  \end{quote} 
 For  each symbol $\s\in \Sigma\langle E, F \rangle $,  we  
pad the binary form of its number
with leading zeros to length  $\ell$. The binary sequence  obtained in this way is called 
the binary representation 
of  the  symbol $\s\in \Sigma\langle E, F \rangle $, and is  denoted as $\phi(\s)$.
Similarly,  for  each constant $a\in  C\langle E, F \rangle  $, we  
pad the  binary form of its number
with leading zeros to length  $\ell$. The binary sequence  obtained in this way is called 
the binary representation 
of  the constant $a\in  C\langle E, F \rangle  $, and is  denoted as $\phi(a)$.

The binary representation of a 
rule $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) \dot{\rightarrow} a\in R\langle E, F \rangle$ is the string  $\phi(\s)\phi(a_1), \ldots, \phi(a_m)\phi(a)$. 
We store the binary representations of the  rewrite rules in $R\langle E, F \rangle$  without duplicates in the   red-black tree  $RBT1$ in lexicographic order.
 The binary representation of an ordered pair  $(a, b)\in BSTEP\langle E ,  F \rangle$ is the ordered pair $(\phi(a), \phi(b))$. 
We store the binary representations of 
the elements of  $BSTEP\langle E, F \rangle$ without duplicates in the   red-black tree  $RBT2$ in lexicographic order.
Here 
for every ordered pair 
$(a, b)\in BSTEP\langle E , F\rangle$, the search key is the first component $\phi(a)$, the second component, $\phi(b)$ is  satellite data.\newline
 {\bf var}  $i$, $m$: $\Nat$; $\s$:  $\Sigma$; $a, a_1, \ldots, a_{mar_{\S}}: C \langle E,  F  \rangle$; $RBT1$, $RBT2$: red-black tree;
