\begin{lem}\label{eso} Let 
 $E$ and $F$ be  GTESs over  a signature  $\Sigma$. 
 \newline
  1. Algorithm CAD on $\Sigma$, $E$, and $F$ produces    
  \begin{itemize}
\item the relations  $\rho\langle E, F\rangle$, $\rho\langle F, E\rangle$,  and $\rho\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item the  set of nullary symbols  $C\langle E, F\rangle$, $C\langle F, E\rangle$, and
$C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
\item  $INC( E\cup F, E) $ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree   saved in a  variable  $RBT1$;
\item  $INC( E\cup F, F)$ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable    $RBT2$;
\item $NUM(E\cup F, E)$ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable    $RBT3$;
\item $NUM(E\cup F, F)$ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable    $RBT4$;
\item the GTRS $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$  stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in a  variable  $RBT5$,
 each node  in  $RBT5$ contains a search key  which is  a rule in $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$, and satellite data $counter_E$ and $counter_F$;
 \item $BSTEP\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in a  variable   $RBT6$; 
for every ordered pair$(a, b)\in BSTEP\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$, the search key is the first component $a$, the second component, $b$ is  satellite data;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in a  variable  $RBT7$;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle  F, E\rangle$ stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable   $RBT8$; 
 \item 
the auxiliary dpwpa $ AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle, A[E; F])$ for the GTESs $E$ and $F$.
\end{itemize}  

 2.    Algorithm CAD on $\Sigma$, $E$, and $F$  decides
 for every constant $a\in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$, 
 whether  $a\in C\langle E, F \rangle$ and whether  $a\in C\langle F, E \rangle $, and  
    sets the value of the attribute $a.equal_E$ to true if and only if $a\in C\langle E, F \rangle$, and sets the value of the attribute $a.equal_F$ to true if and only if $a\in C\langle F, E \rangle$.


   3.  Algorithm CAD on $\Sigma$, $E$, and $F$ decides for any $c\in  R\langle {E \cup F}, \emptyset\rangle$ and any rewrite rule 
 $\s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c\in  R\langle {E \cup F}, \emptyset \rangle$ with right-hand side $c$,
 whether  for all $a_1, \ldots, a_m  \in C\langle E, F\rangle$  such that 
 $a_i \subseteq  c_i$ for every  $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$, 
 there exists a  rewrite rule  in $R\langle E, F \rangle$ with left-hand side 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m) $. If so, then it sets  the value of the attribute $c.keeps_E$ to $\mathrm{true}$, otherwise it sets 
 the value of the attribute $c.keeps_E$ to $\mathrm{false}$.


   4. Algorithm CAD on $\Sigma$, $E$, and $F$ decides for any   $c\in  R\langle {E \cup F}, \emptyset \rangle$ and any rewrite rule 
 $\s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c\in  R\langle {E \cup F}, \emptyset\rangle$ with right-hand side $c$,
     whether  for all $b_1, \ldots, b_m  \in C\langle F, E\rangle$  such that 
 $b_i \subseteq  c_i$ for every  $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$, 
 there exists a  rewrite rule  in $R\langle F, E \rangle$ with left-hand side 
 $\s(b_1, \ldots, b_m) $. If so, then it sets  the value of the attribute $c.keeps_F$ to $\mathrm{true}$, otherwise it sets 
 the value of the attribute $c.keeps_F$ to $\mathrm{false}$. 

  \end{lem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition \ref{kecskemet} and by direct inspection of Definition \ref{augbarack}, we obtain Statement 1. 

By Proposition \ref{lezaras},  we know the cardinality of all  $W/_{\Theta\langle E, F \rangle}$-classes,  $W/_{\Theta\langle F, E\rangle}$-classes, and 
 $W/_{\Theta\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle}$-classes. For all  $a \in R\langle E,F  \rangle$
 and 
  $c \in R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset  \rangle$ such that $a\cap c \neq \emptyset$, we have $a=c$ if and only if $|a|=|c|$.
  Hence we have Statement 2.

We now show Statement 3.
 For every  rewrite rule $\s(c_1, \ldots, c_m)\dot{\rightarrow} c \in R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset  \rangle$, we decide 
whether for all $a_1, \ldots, a_m\in C\langle E, F\rangle$ with  $a_1\subseteq c_1, \ldots, a_m\subseteq c_m$,
there exists a rewrite rule with left-hand side 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$ in $R\langle E, F \rangle$.  When constructing the 
 GTRS $R\langle E, F \rangle$, whenever we add to  $R\langle E, F \rangle$ a new rule with left-hand side $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$,
 where $a_1\subseteq a_1, \ldots, a_m\subseteq c_m$,
 we add $1$ to the
 attribute $ \s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c.counter_E $. 
  In this way, we count the rules  in $R\langle  E, F\rangle$  with left-hand side 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$,
  where $a_1\subseteq c_1, \ldots, a_m\subseteq c_m$,
   and store the result in the attribute $ \s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c.counter_E $. 
  Then $\s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c.counter_E  =\Pi_{i=1} ^ m |c_i/_{\Theta \langle E, F\rangle}|$ if and only if 
  for all $a_1, \ldots, a_m\in C\langle E, F\rangle$
 with  $a_1\subseteq c_1, \ldots, a_m\subseteq c_m$,
 there exists a  rewrite rule with left-hand side $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$
 in $ R\langle E, F \rangle$. 
   When Algorithm   CAD terminates,  for every vertex $c\in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$,
\begin{quote}  $ c.keeps_E=\mathrm{true}$ if and only if \newline 
 for every rewrite rule  of the form 
 $\s(c_1, \ldots, c_m) \dot{\rightarrow} c\in  R\langle {E \cup F}, \emptyset \rangle$, \newline
 for every  $a_1, \ldots, a_m \in C\langle E, F\rangle$ with $a_i \subseteq  c_i$
 for all $i\in \{\, 1, \ldots, m\, \}$,  \newline
  there exists a rewrite rule in $ R\langle E, F\rangle$ of the form 
 $\s(a_1, \ldots, a_m )\dot{\rightarrow} a $ for some $a\in C\langle E, F\rangle$.
\end{quote}

The proof of Satement 4 is similar to that of Statement 3.
  \end{proof}

  \begin{lem}\label{bonn} Let 
 $E$ and $F$ be  GTESs over  a signature  $\Sigma$. 
Algorithm CAD runs in $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$   time  on $\Sigma$, $E$, and $F$, 
  where $n= size(E)+size(F)$.  \end{lem}

 \begin{proof} The proof consists of showing the following nine   statements:

  1. The time complexity of constructing the red-black trees $RBT1$ and $RBT2$ is $O(n)$. 

  2.   The time complexity of constructing  the relation $\tau\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$ 
and  the  GTES $E \cup F$  stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in the  variable  $RBT5$,
  is  $O(n^2)$ time.

3. The time complexity of computing the value attributes  $a.equal_E$ and  $a.equal_F$ for all constants $a\in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$, is $O(n)$. 

 4. The time complexity of constructing the following data is  $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$.
 \begin{itemize} 
\item the GTRS $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$  stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in the  variable  $RBT5$,
 each node  in  $RBT5$ contains a search key  which is  a rule in $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle$;
 \item $BSTEP\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ stored in a red-black tree saved in the  variable   $RBT6$; 
   \item the GTRS $R\langle E, F\rangle$ stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree saved in the  variable  $RBT7$;
  \item the GTRS $R\langle  F, E\rangle$ stored without  duplicate values in a red-black tree  saved in the  variable   $RBT8$; 
 \end{itemize}

5.   The time complexity of constructing
\begin{itemize}
\item $NUM( E\cup F, E)$ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable    $RBT3$, and 
\item $NUM( E\cup F, F)$ stored  without  duplicate values  in a red-black tree  saved in a  variable    $RBT4$
\end{itemize} 
is $O(n)$. 


 6. The time complexitity  of computing the values of 
 the   satellite data $counter_E$, and $counter_F$ for all rules in $R\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$
  is $O(n)$. 


 7. The time complexity of constructing the 
 auxiliary dpwpa $ AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset\rangle, A[E; F])$
  is $O(n)$.


 8.   The time complexitity  of computing the values of  the  attributes $equal_E$ 
 and $equal_F$   of  all constants $a\in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$ is   $O(n)$. 


 9. The time complexitity  of computing the values of the  attributes  $keeps_E$ and 
  $keeps_F$ of  all constants $a\in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$  is  $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$ time. 



  First we now show Statement 1.  
  By Statement \ref{csorvas},   $INC( E\cup F, E)leq n $. Consequently, 
when we construct the  red-black tree $RBT1$, we carry out at most $n$ insertion. By Proposition \ref{redblack}, 
  we construct the  red-black tree $RBT1$ in $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$ time. 
 Similarly, we construct the  red-black tree $RBT2$ in $O(n\, \mathrm{log}\, n)$ time. 



 Second, by Statement \ref{erre}, we have Statement 2.    





 Third, we carry out $2n$ comparisons of nonnegative numbers in $O(n)$ time. 





Fourth, By Statement 2 and Statement 3 of \ref{kecskemet}, we have Statement 4.



Fifth, we now show Statement 5.
When we construct the  red-black tree $RBT3$, we read the whole red-black tree $RBT1$ in search key order in $O(n)$ time. Each  modification takes 
constant  time.
By Statement \ref{csorvas},   $|INC( E\cup F, E)|\leq n $.   Consequently, we construct the  red-black tree $RBT3$ in $O(n)$ time. 
 Similarly, we construct the  red-black tree $RBT4$ in $O(n)$ time. 


   Sixth,  we  now show Statement 6. 
 When Algorithm CAD computes the values of the  satellite data  
    $counter_E$ and $counter_F$ for all rules of  the GTRS $R\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle$,  
    it carries out  at most $2n$  additions, since
   $|  C\langle E, F\rangle|\leq    |W|\leq n$ and       $|  C\langle F, E\rangle|\leq    |W|\leq n$.   


  Seventh we now show Statement 7. 
 We build the adjacency-list representation of the dpwpa    $ AUX[ E;  F] =
