\begin{lem}\label{supportfut}{\em \cite{cormen}} Let $E$ and $F$ be GTESs over a unary  signature   $\Sigma$.
 Algorithm NPDFS runs on $AUX[ E;  F] $  in $O(n^2)$ time.
 \end{lem}
\begin{proof} Recall that  $n=size(E)+size(F)$.
 By Statement \ref{darabsz}, 
$|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | \leq n$. By   Statement \ref{iszap},  
  $| A[E; F]|\leq n$.
    The running time of depth first search on   $AUX[ E;  F] $ is $O(|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | + | A[E; F]|)$   
\cite[Section~22.3]{cormen}. Consequently, the running time of depth first search on   $ AUX[ E;  F] $ is $O(n)$.


The outer loop of 
 Algorithm NPDFS on  $AUX[ E;  F] $ implements a prefix of 
the depth first search  on $AUX[ E;  F] $  \cite{cormen}, it can terminate before visiting all vertices of $ AUX[ E;  F] $.
Consequently, the outer loop  on   $AUX[ E;  F] $ takes  $O(n)$ time.


Function  INNER-LOOP-E  on  $AUX[ E;  F] $ and any  vertex  $a$
 implements a prefix of a depth first search    on $ AUX[ E;  F] $ starting from $a$. 
Accordingly the running time of function INNER-LOOP-E is $O(n)$.  
 Similarly, the running time of  function INNER-LOOP-F is
 $O(n)$.  
 By  Statement \ref{darabsz}, $|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | \leq n$, therefore
INNER-LOOP-E and INNER-LOOP-F run at most $n$ times. Accordingly,   Algorithm NPDFS runs on   $ AUX[ E;  F] $  in $O(n^2)$ time.
    \end{proof}

