 \subsection{Partial Depth First Search  on the Auxiliary  dpwpa  }


     We now define a partial depth first search on the auxiliary dpwpa   
$AUX[ E;  F] =
(C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle, A[E; F])$, which is
 a restricted and slightly modified verison of the depth first search  on graphs \cite{cormen}.
We  start the partial depth first search 
from the elements  of $C(E\cup F, \emptyset)\setminus C(E, F)$.
 \begin{df}\label{lo} \rm Let   $\Sigma$ be a signature such that 
   $\S_k\neq \emptyset$ for some $k\geq 2$, and let  $E$ and $F$ be GTESs over   $\Sigma$. \newline
   Algorithm Partial Depth First Search on Auxiliary dpwpa $AUX[ E;  F]  $  (PDFS for short){\rm \newline
 Input: the auxiliary dpwpa  $ AUX[ E;  F] $.
 Output: Boolean. \newline
\{ Output is true if GTRS $R\langle E, F \rangle$ and GTRS $R\langle F, E \rangle$ together  simulate the  GTRS $R\langle E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle$, false otherwise. \}\newline
{\bf var}   $a: C \langle E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle$;   
\begin{lstlisting}
(*$\mathrm{PDFS}( G \langle  E, F \rangle): \; \mathrm{Boolean}$*) 
  (*{\bf for each} vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$ {\bf do} *) 
    (*{\bf begin}*)
      (*{\bf if} {\bf not}  $a.equal_E$ {\bf and}  {\bf not}  $a.equal_F$*)
        (*{\bf then} {\bf return}\; $\mathrm{false}$*)
      (*\{\, if $a\not\in C\langle  E, F \rangle $ \mbox{ and }$ a\not \in C\langle  F, E \rangle $ \mbox{ then GTRS } $R\langle E, F \rangle$ \mbox{ and GTRS } $R\langle F, E \rangle$ \mbox{ together do not  simulate the  GTRS }  $R\langle E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle$. \}*)
      (*$a.visited:=\mathrm{false}$*)
    (*{\bf end}*) 
  (*{\bf for each} vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle$ {\bf do}*) 
    (*{\bf if} {\bf not} $a.equal_E$ and {\bf not} $a.visited$*)   
    (*\{\ if    $a\in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle\setminus C(E, F)$ and $a$ has not been visited yet \}*)
      (*${\bf then }$ ${\bf for}$ ${\bf  each}$ vertex $b\in Adj(a)$ \bf{do}*)
         (*${\bf if }$ {\bf not} $b.visited$*)  
           (*${\bf then}$ if {\bf not} $\mathrm{PDFS-VISIT}( AUX[ E;  F] , b)$ {\bf then}*) 
             (*${\bf return}\; \mathrm{false}$*)  
  (*$ {\bf output}(\mathrm{true})$*)        



(*${\bf function} \;\mathrm{PDFS-VISIT}(AUX[ E;  F] , a): \; \mathrm{Boolean}$*)    
  (*$a.visited:=\mathrm{true}$;*)
  (*{\bf if} $ a.keeps_F=\mathrm{true}$*)   
    (*${\bf then }$ {\bf begin}*)
      (*{\bf for} {\bf  each} vertex $b\in Adj(a)$ \bf{do}*)
        (*${\bf if }$ {\bf not} $b.visited$*) 
          (*${\bf then}$ ${\bf if}$ ${\bf not}$ $\mathrm{PDFS-VISIT}(AUX[ E;  F] , b)$*) 
            (*${\bf then}$ ${\bf return}$ $ \mathrm{false}$*)
    (*{\bf end}*)   
  (*${\bf else }\; {\bf return}\; \mathrm{false}$*)
  (*$ {\bf output}(\mathrm{true})$*)   
 \end{lstlisting}}
\end{df}
When  Algorithm PDFS starts the search from a vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle\setminus C(E, F)$, it does not set the attribute $a.visited $ to true. 
 Consequently if for this  vertex $a\in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle\setminus C(E, F)$, there exists    a cycle leading from the vertex $a$ to itself, 
   then  Algorithm PDFS visits vertex $a$ twice. 

We run Algorithm PDFS on the auxiliary dpwpa  $ AUX[ E;  F] $. We shall show that PDFS   on $AUX[ E;  F] $ outputs true  if and only if 
GTRS $R\langle E, F \rangle$ and GTRS $R\langle F, E \rangle$ 
together  simulate the  GTRS $R\langle E \cup F, \emptyset \rangle$.


\begin{lem}\label{gezenguz}{\em \cite{cormen}}   Let $\S$ be a  signature   such that
   $\S_k\neq \emptyset$ for some $k\geq 2$,   and let 
 $E$ and $F$ be  GTESs over   $\Sigma$ such that $R\langle E, F\rangle$ is total.
  Let $a\in  C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle  \setminus  C\langle E, F \rangle$.

(i)  During the run of Algorithm PDFS on $AUX[ E;  F] $, Function $\mathrm{PDFS-VISIT}$ on $( AUX[ E;  F] , a)$ 
 visits at most once those vertices $b \in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle $ such that
 there is a path of positive length or a  cycle  from 
  $a$  to $b$. 

  (ii) During the run of Algorithm PDFS on $AUX[ E;  F] $, Function $\mathrm{PDFS-VISIT}$ visits at most once  those vertices $b \in C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle $ such that
 there is a path of positive length or a  cycle  from some vertex
  $a  \in C\langle E\cup F, \emptyset \rangle\setminus C\langle E, F \rangle $ to $b$. 
\end{lem} 
\begin{proof} We obtain Statement (i) by direct inspection of Definition \ref{lo}. Statement (i) implies Statement (ii).
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{fut}{\em \cite{cormen}}  Let $\S$ be a  signature   such that
   $\S_k\neq \emptyset$ for some $k\geq 2$,   and let 
 $E$ and $F$ be  GTESs over   $\Sigma$ such that $R\langle E, F\rangle$ is total.
 Algorithm PDFS  runs on $AUX[ E;  F] $  in $O(n)$ time.
 \end{prop}
\begin{proof} 
The running time of
PDFS is $O(|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | + |A[E; F]|)$ \cite{cormen}. Recall that  $n=size(E)+size(F)$. 
By Definition \ref{szeder} and Statement \ref{drrszam}, 
 $|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | \leq |W|\leq n$.   
  By Statement \ref{iszap}  
  $  | A[E; F]|\leq n$. Hence $|C\langle E \cup F, \emptyset\rangle | + |A[E; F]|\leq 2n$. Then   
   we have the proposition.
 \end{proof}


