\subsection{Agent Action Space}
Each agent has two action spaces depending on the effect of a selected action on the environment: the communication action does not have direct impact on the environment whereas the environment action directly affects the environment.

In each time step $t$, the environment action which the $m_{th}$ AP can take is given by $a^{m}_{t} \in \{0,1\}$  where $0$ means AP does nothing, $1$ means AP transmits command carrying packet.
Similarly, the communication actions of the $m_{th}$ AP are control messages sent to the CC in the uplink and is given by $U^{m}_{t} = \{0,1\}$ where $0$ means AP sends Power Allocation Request (PAR), $1$ means AP sends CSI report. Due to the overhead incurred by frequent CSI reports, the AP agents will need to learn when using this communication action is pertinent. 

Similarly, the CC also sends communication messages to each $m$ AP given by $D^{m}_{t} = \{0,...,1\}$ where  $\{0,..,1\}$ indicates the coefficient of power allocation. The CC being an expert agent understands the meaning of both uplink and downlink communication messages. However, each AP agent will need to learn how to use the uplink communication messages based on its interaction with the environment. 

