\subsection{Full Matrix Inversion Performance}

This section examines the performance of \textit{sTiles} in full matrix inversion and compares it against PLASMA \cite{agullo2009plasma, agullo2011towards}, highlighting the impact of scheduling strategies. PLASMA employs dynamic scheduling, while \textit{sTiles} uses static scheduling to increase data locality and minimize scheduling overhead.

Figures \ref{fig:full_dense_plasma} and \ref{fig:full_dense_stiles} present the execution times of both libraries across different tile sizes and core counts for matrix ID 5 from Table \ref{tab:matrix_properties_stiles1}. The main observations are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{sTiles} consistently outperforms PLASMA, achieving lower execution times across all configurations.
    \item PLASMA suffers from performance degradation beyond 16 cores, particularly for small tile sizes (40 and 80). This is attributed to dynamic scheduling overhead and thread contention.
    \item \textit{sTiles} scales efficiently, maintaining a steady decrease in execution time up to 52 cores, demonstrating better parallel efficiency.
    \item Tile size sensitivity is lower in sTiles, meaning it achieves strong performance across different tile sizes without requiring extensive tuning.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure} \centering \includegraphics[width=0.9\columnwidth]{full_dense_plasma.png} \caption{Execution times of PLASMA for full matrix inversion across different tile sizes and core counts for matrix ID 5.} \label{fig:full_dense_plasma} \end{figure}

