We next show a bound on the number of nodes that arrive within an interval of time (proof in Section \ref{sec:arrivalproof}).

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:arrival}
    Let $N(t', t)$ be the number of nodes arriving in the interval $[t', t]$ in the Poisson churn model with rate $\lambda = 1$
    and stable network size $n$. Then, $E[N(t',t)] = t-t' = n'$ and  $N(t',t)$ is well concentrated around $n'$:
        $$Pr\left(n' - O(\sqrt{n'\ln n}) \leq N(t,t') \leq n' + O(\sqrt{n'\ln n})\right) \geq 1-  \frac{1}{n^2}.$$
\end{lemma}

