Note from the definition \ref{def: core}, the core set $\core$ will be fixed throughout phase $P_{t',t}$. Thus, we reduce the dynamic graph to have a static core subgraph over which we can analyze the random walks. Hence, we can appeal to Theorem 2.1 of \cite{soda25}  to obtain the following result. This theorem says that most random walks started by honest nodes in the core subgraph will walk only in the core subgraph and will mix nearly uniformly at random in the core.
Specifically, most of the $\tot$ tokens created by most of the honest nodes in core will walk
$2\texttt{rwLength}$ steps in the core (time taken to reach its destination in $\texttt{rwLength}$
steps and then walk back in reverse after verification). Furthermore, the number of steps taken to complete 
these $2\texttt{rwLength}$ steps will be $O(\log n)$ with high probability. Further, each of these tokens has a nearly uniform probability
of $\Theta(1/|V_t|)$ of reaching any particular node in the core.


