\section{Application}
\subsection{Byzantine Agreement in Dynamic Expander Graphs}
\label{sec:bae}

Our protocol can be used to implement a fully-distributed Byzantine Agreement algorithm in a dynamic setting. We adapt the algorithm from Augustine et al. \cite{podc13}, which worked in a dynamic network with adversarial churn and presence of $$. In our setting, the core subgraph, $\core$ formed in each phase is an expander and contains honest nodes with high probability. Each node begins with an input bit and sends out $\Theta(\log^2 n)$ verified random walks using our Algorithm \ref{alg:byzantineSamplingSparse}. These walks carry the node's current bit and ask the destination nodes to send it back. Once a node receives response from all its walks, it takes a majority and updates its bit accordingly. This process is repeated for $\Theta(\log n)$ rounds. Since each phase lasts $\eta \log n$ rounds and the core remains unchanged during this time with high probability, one round of the algorithm can be simulated in each phase. The verified walks ensure that most messages come from honest nodes. As a result, this approach allows the agreement algorithm from Augustine et al. \cite{podc13} to work in our setting with dynamic churn and in the presence of Byzantine nodes. 

We include the Theorem \ref{thm:BAE}, adapted from Augustine et al. \cite{podc13}, to show that the Byzantine Agreement algorithm they designed can be directed applied to our dynamic core subgraph. Since our protocol ensures that each phase has a large expander core with mostly honest nodes and stable topology for $\Theta(\log n)$ rounds, their analysis still holds and applies in our setting, showing that agreement can be reached in $O(\log^3 n)$ rounds with high probability.
