\begin{abstract}
%The Advanced Metering Infrastructure (AMI) enables more granular load forecasting, which is essential for grid stability, optimal operations, energy management, etc. While Deep Learning (DL) models can efficiently extract features from smart meter data and capture non-linear dependencies, loads' complex dynamics and non-stationarity remain challenging, requiring models that adapt to varying conditions. To enhance adaptability, heterogeneous external data (e.g., weather, calendar effects, and demand response signals) provide valuable contexts. However, past work treated them as direct inputs, which limits effectiveness due to their varying scales and dynamics. For example, DL models will focus on volatile load variations rather than smooth weather trends or discrete-time labels, failing to extract useful information from external data. To address this, we propose a novel and unified framework that effectively processes heterogeneous external data to enhance the accuracy of load forecasting. The core idea is to treat external data as meta-knowledge, which guides how the base DL model is formulated, while the base model focuses solely on historical load data to extract effective temporal patterns. This meta-representation module runs in parallel to handle diverse external data sources, with each component processed using Layer Normalization (LN) to ensure a balanced adaptation of the DL model to different external conditions. In addition, to remove redundancy and avoid overfitting in the face of a large number of external sources, we employ the so-called Mixture-of-Expert (MoE) model to select the most important meta-representations. In general, our framework is a plug-and-play module that efficiently makes arbitrary DL models adaptable to the environments, increasing their performances. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed approach significantly improves model adaptability, leading to substantial performance gains over various baselines in both deterministic and probabilistic load forecasting.

%\begin{abstract}
Accurate residential load forecasting is critical for power system reliability with rising renewable integration and demand-side flexibility. However, most statistical and machine learning models treat external factors, such as weather, calendar effects, and pricing, as extra input, ignoring their heterogeneity, and thus limiting the extraction of useful external information. We propose a paradigm shift: external data should serve as meta-knowledge to dynamically adapt the forecasting model itself. Based on this idea, we design a meta-representation framework using hypernetworks that modulate selected parameters of a base Deep Learning (DL) model in response to external conditions. This provides both expressivity and adaptability. %We further integrate a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) mechanism to enable robust, context-dependent expert selection, and introduce residual parameterization to support fast, stable inference. 
We further integrate a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) mechanism to enhance efficiency through selective expert activation, while improving robustness by filtering redundant external inputs. %Residual parameterization is introduced to support fast and stable inference.
The resulting model, dubbed as a Meta Mixture of Experts for External data (\text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}), achieves substantial improvements in accuracy and robustness with limited additional overhead, outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods in diverse load datasets. The dataset and source code are publicly available at \href{https://github.com/haorandd/M2oE2_load_forecast.git} {\textcolor{blue}{https://github.com/haorandd/M2oE2\_load\_forecast.git}}.



%Accurate load forecasting under diverse and dynamic conditions is critical for reliable power system operation. However, most deep learning (DL) models treat external factors, such as holidays, pricing signals, and weather—as static inputs to a fixed forecasting model, limiting their ability to adapt to context-sensitive behavioral changes. In this paper, we propose a meta-representation framework that treats external data as high-level context to dynamically adjust the model’s forecasting behavior. This decoupled design enables the forecasting model to generalize across operating conditions without retraining. To achieve efficiency, we introduce a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) structure that activates only the most relevant model components at inference time, reducing computational overhead. To ensure robustness, we incorporate a residual path that serves as a fallback in uncertain conditions, preventing degradation in accuracy. This unified architecture allows any base DL model to be adapted for heterogeneous environments. Experiments on real-world residential load datasets show that our method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, particularly under anomalous or infrequent external conditions, while maintaining fast and reliable inference.
\end{abstract}




