\section{Introduction}


%Load forecasting, a long-standing and fundamental topic in power systems, plays a critical role in various aspects, such as economic dispatch \cite{kalakova2021novel,han2021task}, demand response \cite{chen2017short,pramono2019deep}, grid stability/reliability analysis \cite{habbak2023load,saxena2024intelligent}, event identification \cite{ref:Haoran2022D,ref:Haoran2022T,ref:Haoran2023S}, and infrastructure planning \cite{carvallo2016load,zhang2023novel}. Accurate forecasting enhances operational efficiency, reduces costs, and improves reliability, making it indispensable in modern power grids.

%\paragraph{Motivation.} 
Load forecasting is a long-standing and fundamental task in power systems, underpinning key operations such as economic dispatch~\cite{han2021task}, demand response~\cite{pramono2019deep}, grid stability and reliability analysis~\cite{saxena2024intelligent}, event detection~\cite{ref:Haoran2022D,ref:Haoran2022T,ref:Haoran2023S}, and infrastructure planning~\cite{zhang2023novel}. With increasing smart meters, there is a growing shift toward bottom-up residential load forecasting~\cite{ref:2019ChengjinD}. This is because accurate forecasts at the residential level support distribution-level operations, e.g., the coordination of Distributed Energy Resources (DERs), local market participation, and demand-side management~\cite{sajjad2020novel}. Additionally, load forecast also enhances system-wide reliability and efficiency by improving bulk market predictions and capacity planning~\cite{ref:Ponoćko2018F}. %In particular, probabilistic forecasting plays an increasingly critical role, as it quantifies uncertainty for risk-aware decision-making~\cite{wang2018combining,yang2019bayesian}, enabling grid operators and market participants to better manage renewable integration and flexible demand scenarios.


%Recently, the widespread deployment of smart meters has enabled bottom-up forecasting \cite{ref:2019ChengjinD}, such as high-granular residential load forecasting. Accurate bottom-level load forecasts benefit not only distribution-level planning and operations, such as Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) management, demand response, and local energy markets \cite{sajjad2020novel,hafeez2020innovative}, but also bulk power markets by enhancing system-wide demand prediction, grid stability, and capacity planning \cite{ref:Ponoćko2018F}. In particular, probabilistic load forecasting can quantify uncertainty for risk-aware decision-making \cite{wang2018combining,yang2019bayesian}. Therefore, grid operators and market participants can better anticipate demand fluctuations, optimize resource allocation, and enhance system resilience in the face of increasing renewable energy penetration and demand-side flexibility.

%\paragraph{Metric Needs and Challenges.} 
Despite its importance, highly granular load forecasting remains challenging due to high volatility and non-stationarity. Unlike aggregated loads, residential usage patterns are heavily influenced by diverse factors such as occupant behavior, appliance schedules, weather, and the growing integration of rooftop photovoltaics and electric vehicles, all of which contribute to complex and irregular temporal dynamics~\cite{kong2017short}. %Moreover, there is an increasing need for \emph{probabilistic forecasting} arises from the requirement to quantify and manage uncertainty. this is an essential capability for risk-aware operational decisions in the presence of distributed and stochastic energy resources~\cite{wang2018combining,yang2019bayesian}. 
To gain accurate forecasts, a promising approach is to effectively incorporate heterogeneous external information, such as weather conditions, calendar features, and demand response (DR) signals~\cite{wang2018review}. However, these external factors often vary in data type (continuous vs. categorical), exhibit weak or delayed correlations with the load, and require specialized treatment for meaningful integration into forecasting models.

