%However, this task remains challenging due to the high volatility and non-stationarity of residential load patterns. Factors such as weather variability, occupant behavior, appliance usage diversity, and the growing integration of DERs introduce complex temporal dependencies and uncertainties \cite{kong2017short}. Additionally, effectively incorporating heterogeneous external data, such as meteorological conditions, calendar effects, and Demand Response (DR) signals, poses integration challenges \cite{wang2018review}. Addressing these challenges requires advanced models that can capture complex load dynamics while adapting to varying environmental conditions.

%\paragraph{Limitations of Past Philosophies.}
Therefore, a large body of work aims to improve residential load forecasting through increasingly sophisticated modeling techniques. Nevertheless, most of them share a common design flaw: treating all available data, including external information, as static inputs to a single fixed model. Such a design overlooks the contextual role that external factors can play in dynamically modulating load behavior. For example, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:one_week_data}, we perform normalization to make data from different sources comparable. However, external data are relatively flat compared to highly variable load curves. Therefore, in terms of extracting the temporal trends and patterns, a forecaster will pay great attention to historical load data and nearly ignore external information. In addition, given diverse external sources, it remains challenging to simultaneously process them. The model may suffer from overfitting issues when incorporating too many external datasets with high dimensionality and redundancy. 



These limitations have been observed in existing approaches, which can be categorized into three groups. The first employs classical statistical or Machine Learning (ML) techniques, such as ARIMA, SARIMA, exponential smoothing~\cite{christiaanse1971short}, Gaussian process \cite{weng2015probabilistic,xie2017adaptive,xie2018integrated,weng2018probabilistic,xie2020input}, and kernel regressor~\cite{abbasi2019short}, which rely on handcrafted features and struggle with high-dimensional, nonlinear load dynamics~\cite{lu2024short}. These models often treat contextual information as fixed covariates without modeling how they influence the forecasting function itself. 

The second group leverages Deep Learning (DL) models, e.g., Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) \cite{che2018recurrent}, Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)~\cite{kong2017short,xie2021enhance}, and Transformers~\cite{l2022transformer}, to automatically extract hierarchical features from smart meter data, often outperforming classical methods by capturing complex temporal dependencies. Hybrid architectures like Convolutional Neural Network (CNN-)LSTM models~\cite{dong2022ultra} further improve representation learning by combining local and sequential patterns, but still treat external signals as additive inputs \cite{cao2025short,zeng2021uncovering}. The third group introduces physics-informed ML models, which encode physical constraints related to building systems~\cite{chen2023physics}, grid dynamics~\cite{li2022physics}, or battery behaviors~\cite{xu2022physics}. These methods are less applicable to residential loads where physical equations may be unavailable. %The fourth group attempts to integrate external information—e.g., weather, calendar effects, or socioeconomic variables—directly as auxiliary inputs to the neural network~. While intuitive, this approach often leads to limited gains, as the model tends to ignore weak external signals or overfits when high-dimensional contextual features are included indiscriminately.



%Extensive work has been done in this domain and can be categorized into the following groups. The first group employs statistical or classical Machine Learning (ML) techniques, which leverage time series models (e.g., ARIMA, SARIMA, exponential smoothing \cite{christiaanse1971short}) and feature-based regression methods (e.g., Support Vector Regression, Random Forests, XGBoost \cite{abbasi2019short}) to forecast load. These approaches rely on historical consumption patterns and engineered features to capture seasonality and trend dependencies. However, these methods struggle with non-linearity and the increasing dimensionality of residential load data \cite{lu2024short}. 

%\paragraph{Philosophy and Meta-Representation Design.}
In this paper, we propose to treat external information as \emph{meta-knowledge} that dynamically changes the forecasting model, rather than modeling it as an additional input. Specifically, we employ \emph{hypernetworks}~\cite{ha2016hypernetworks} to modulate a compact subset of parameters in the base network based on contextual signals. This meta-representation allows the model to adjust its behavior in response to evolving external conditions, such as weather, calendar effects, and occupant behaviors. To ensure numerical stability and balanced influence across heterogeneous data sources, we apply Layer Normalization~\cite{xiong2020layer} to each meta-representation. Moreover, we carefully constrain the parameter modulation to a low-dimensional, high-impact subset of weights to maintain computational efficiency, which is a significant extension of our previous work \cite{li2025exarnn}.

In addition to the high model adaptation and moderate efficiency, our architecture naturally integrates enhancements such as robustness and plug-and-play properties without requiring major architectural changes. To improve \emph{robustness}, we introduce a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) structure~\cite{zhou2022mixture}, where each expert corresponds to a distinct external source. A learned gating mechanism dynamically selects a sparse subset of active experts, enabling the model to suppress irrelevant or redundant signals and focus on the informative context. To ensure that our meta-framework always enhances the base forecaster, we adopt \emph{residual parameter modulation}. The mechanism guarantees that external information is always employed to reduce the residual errors of a base model. Consequently, the model's accuracy keeps increasing as long as we can introduce a more accurate base forecaster. While some related frameworks emerged in $2025$, they typically integrate MoE or residual ideas in an ad-hoc fashion \cite{tsoumplekas2025few,huang2025metaeformer}. In contrast, our architecture was designed to support modularity and scalability in modern load forecasting environments.









%Additionally, with certain prior physical knowledge, the third group develops physics-informed DL models to enhance prediction performance. These methods incorporate domain-specific constraints, such as thermodynamic models for Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC) loads \cite{chen2023physics}, grid stability equations \cite{li2022physics}, or battery charge- discharge dynamics, into neural networks \cite{xu2022physics}. However, these approaches rely on well-defined physical models and require careful integration of domain knowledge, which is often unclear for residential loads. Finally, to achieve effective adaptation to external conditions, the fourth group fuses load measurements with external data, such as weather conditions, temporal indicators (day of the week, month), demand response signals, and socioeconomic factors \cite{cao2025short}. For instance, temperature and humidity significantly affect residential heating and cooling loads \cite{cao2021impacts}, while time-related features help capture seasonal variations \cite{zeng2021uncovering}. However, most existing work integrates external data as additional inputs to the DL model. However, the effectiveness of this integration remains uncertain.

%\paragraph{Empirical Evidence.}
The overall model is dubbed as a Meta Mixture of Experts for External data (\text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}). We conduct extensive experiments on multiple real-world residential load datasets to evaluate the effectiveness of \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}. These datasets span different geographic regions, temporal resolutions, and weather patterns, ensuring a diverse and realistic testbed. We benchmark our approach against state-of-the-art baselines, including deep learning models with direct external input fusion, attention-based methods, etc. Results demonstrate that \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}} consistently outperforms alternatives in terms of point forecasting accuracy, probabilistic sharpness, and calibration. These empirical findings substantiate our central thesis: that treating external information as meta-knowledge, rather than static inputs, yields more accurate, efficient, and resilient forecasts in complex and dynamic environments.


%We hypothesize that the potential ineffectiveness is due to the difficulty in processing heterogeneous external data, which can be either continuous measurements (such as meteorology data) or discrete labels (such as day types). As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:one_week_data}, we conduct normalization to make inputs of different sources comparable. However, normalized external data are relatively flat compared to the highly variable load curves. Therefore, in terms of extracting the temporal trends and patterns, a sequence DL model (such as an LSTM) will pay high attention to historical load data and nearly ignore external information. In addition, within diverse external sources, it remains challenging to simultaneously understand and process them. Finally, the model may suffer from overfitting issues when incorporating too many external datasets with high dimensionality and redundancy. 



%To properly fuse data, we demand novel \emph{representations}. Intuitively, external data can be cast as meta-knowledge that provides contextual information to describe how the load could possibly evolve. Correspondingly, we develop a novel meta-representation based on hypernetworks \cite{ha2016hypernetworks}, which utilizes external data to determine the evolution of key parameters in a base DL model. These parameters are carefully selected to cause high impacts on the DL model while maintaining a small size to guarantee efficiency. Consequently, the base DL model gains adaptability to different environments. 


%Naturally, we can create multiple meta-representations for different external sources. To avoid numerical issues and enable balanced contributions for each of the representations, we perform Layer Normalization (LN) \cite{xiong2020layer} to maintain a unified range. However, it can be expected that when there are too many external datasets, the meta-representations can be high-dimensional and redundant, making the framework overfitting. While feature engineering technologies can help to select useful features, they are complex and generally introduce additional biases. Therefore, we propose to achieve automatic selection using a Mixture-of-Expert (MoE) model \cite{zhou2022mixture}, where each representation is treated as an expert. At different times, MoE uses a gating mechanism to select a subset of experts to function while filtering out unnecessary ones, which can be easily automated through end-to-end training. Thus, the overfitting issue is largely mitigated. The overall model is dubbed as a Meta Mixture of Experts for External data (\text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}). \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}} can work as a plug-and-play module to make an arbitrary base DL model effectively adaptable to different environments. 



