\subsection{Data Preparations to Train \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}}

%We follow the mini-batch gradient descent \cite{hinton2012neural} to train \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}. While the updating rule in the algorithm is standardized, preparing the mini-batches of training data is task-dependent. For example, we can conduct hourly ($K=1$) or day-ahead load forecast ($K=24$), where $K$ is defined in Subsection \ref{subsec:dl_forecast}. This means that in a mini-batch, the output data should be a 3D array with a size of $N_{\text{batch}}\times K \times d_x$, where $N_{\text{batch}}$ is the number of the training instances in a mini-batch, and we will explain how to build such a batch later. As for the mini-batch input, the length should be sufficiently long to capture the trend, seasonality, and momentum. In this paper, we consider both a fixed length of the previous week's data as well as a variable length from this week's first time slot till the current time. Correspondingly, the consecutive future $K$ samples will be forecast. In general, in a mini-batch, we treat samples every two weeks plus the subsequent $K$ samples as an instance. Although the instance length is fixed, during training, we can easily utilize sliding windows to extract the input and the consecutive output data as described above. To create multiple instances, another sliding window is employed to consecutively cut these chunks. 


\subsubsection{Gathering Mini-Batches via Sliding Windows}
We adopt the mini-batch gradient descent strategy \cite{hinton2012neural} to train \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}}. While the update rule is standardized, the construction of mini-batches is task-dependent. For instance, we may consider either hourly forecasting (\(K = 1\)) or day-ahead forecasting (\(K = 24\)), where \(K\) is the total steps in forecasting, defined in Subsection~\ref{subsec:dl_forecast}. This setup implies that at each time $i$, the output for each mini-batch should be a 3D tensor of size \( N_{\text{batch}} \times  K \times d_x \), where \( N_{\text{batch}} \) is the number of training instances and \( d_x \) is the load dimension. As for the input, the sequence length should be long enough to capture essential temporal patterns, including trend, seasonality, and short-term momentum. In this paper, the input is a combination of: $(1)$ a fixed-length window spanning the previous week's data and $(2)$ a variable-length window extending from the beginning of the current week up to the time $i$. Then, the model predicts the subsequent \(K\) time steps. In general, we construct each training instance \textbf{by extracting a two-week segment of historical data and appending the following \(K\) time steps as the prediction target}. While each instance has a fixed length, we apply a sliding window to \textbf{extract variable input and fixed output pairs} from the time series, where the input and the output are defined above.




%\subsubsection{Grouping A Distribution of Data to Compute Probabilistic Losses} After preparing the training batches, we can compute the losses in Eqs. \eqref{eqn:rnn_mse} or \eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo} for each mini-batch and conduct the gradient backpropagation. In particular, for the probabilistic loss in Eq. \eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}, the computations demand a group of data for $\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}$ that is drawn in a fixed distribution. In particular, recall that we can use the sliding window illustrated in the previous paragraph to obtain input-output pairs. Simultaneously, we can group the output by considering the repeated load trajectories each day and gathering $\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}$ at time $i+1$. Subsequently, the collection of data is used to compute the summand in the right-hand-side of Eq. \eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}. 

%\noindent\textbf{Remark}: as we emphasized in Section \ref{subsec:dl_forecast}, the distribution of $\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}$ is highly complex and depends on the external conditions. For example, the load consumption patterns at noon can be different in weekdays or weekends. Past work requires clustering them into different independent groups to estimate the distributions separately but can easily fail considering diversified external factors. Thanks to the design of our design to integrate the external conditions, \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}} is highly capable of approximating the complex distribution using all data at $i+1$ at once. More specifically, in the training loss in Eq. \eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}, the conditional distribution $p(\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}|\boldsymbol{z}_{i+1},\boldsymbol{h}_i)$ and the posterior $q(\boldsymbol{z}_{i+1}|\boldsymbol{h}_i,\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1})$ of the latent vector are all conditioned on $\boldsymbol{h}_i$ that incorporates all past and effective external data through our meta-representations. In the forecast phase in Eq. \eqref{eqn:prob_forecast2}, such conditions still exist to enable the model to produce different samples for $\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}$ under different conditions.     



%\subsubsection{Grouping Data for Probabilistic Loss Computation} After constructing the training batches, we compute the loss for each mini-batch using either the deterministic objective in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:rnn_mse} or the probabilistic objective in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}, followed by gradient backpropagation. In particular, the probabilistic loss in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo} requires that the output samples \( \boldsymbol{x}_{i+1} \) be drawn from a consistent underlying distribution. To achieve this, we utilize the sliding window technique described earlier to generate input-output pairs, while simultaneously grouping the output data by gathering data at the same time step \( i+1 \) across repeated trajectories. For example, for hourly data,  This grouping forms enable us to compute each summand on the right-hand side of Eq.~\eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}.

%\noindent\textbf{Remark.} As discussed in Section~\ref{subsec:dl_forecast}, the distribution of \( \boldsymbol{x}_{i+1} \) can be highly complex and context-dependent. For example, load consumption patterns can vary significantly between weekdays and weekends. Previous approaches typically rely on clustering or manual partitioning to treat such variations as independent distributions. However, this strategy becomes fragile in the presence of highly diverse or overlapping external factors. In contrast, our proposed \text{M\textsuperscript{2}oE\textsuperscript{2}} framework is designed to model this distributional complexity directly. By conditioning on \( \boldsymbol{z}_{i+1} \) in Eq. \eqref{eqn:rnn_decode}, which encodes both historical dynamics and external information via the meta-representations, our model can accurately approximate the full distribution of \( \boldsymbol{x}_{i+1} \).
%Specifically, in Eq.~\eqref{eqn:rnn_elbo}, both the conditional likelihood \( p(\boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}|\boldsymbol{z}_{i+1}) \) and the posterior \( q(\boldsymbol{z}_{i+1} | \boldsymbol{h}_i, \boldsymbol{x}_{i+1}) \) are context-aware through \( \boldsymbol{h}_i \), enabling adaptive modeling across varying conditions. During inference (Eq.~\eqref{eqn:prob_forecast2}), this context remains available, allowing the model to generate diverse and realistic samples of \( \boldsymbol{x}_{i+1} \) under different external conditions. %{\color{red} Change notations

