\subsection{Audio Tokenization}
\label{subsec:audio_tokenization}
The first step in our method is to convert the raw audio waveform into discrete audio tokens that can be efficiently processed and trained by the DDM.
We utilize a pre-trained audio tokenizer WavTokenizer~\cite{ji2024wavtokenizer}, which compresses high-resolution audio into a compact sequence of discrete tokens using a single quantizer. Despite its extreme compression, WavTokenizer preserves high reconstruction fidelity and rich semantic content. Unlike conventional frameworks for audio inpainting  that operate directly on the audio waveform or the spectrogram \cite{moliner2023diffusion}, audio tokenization allows us to formulate audio inpainting as a discrete sequence completion task.




\subsection{Discrete Diffusion Model}
\label{subsec:ddm}

At the core of our method is a DiT architecture~\cite{peebles2023scalable}, which integrates time conditioning into a standard encoder-only transformer~\cite{vaswani2017attention, devlin2019bert} and following the work of~\cite{lou2310discrete}, we incorporate rotary positional encoding~\cite{su2024roformer}. To operate effectively in the discrete token space, we use the denoising score entropy formulation, guiding the reverse diffusion dynamics as seen in~\ref{eq:dwdse-loss}.


\subsection{Inference Pipeline}
\label{subsec:inference}

At inference time, the input waveform containing a masked audio segment is tokenized using the pretrained \textit{WavTokenizer} encoder. The corrupted region is then inpainted by the trained DDM, which applies reverse diffusion to predict the missing tokens conditioned on the surrounding context. The resulting token sequence is decoded back into waveform space to reconstruct the missing audio. To minimize unnecessary reconstruction loss, we only replace the inpainted segment in the original waveform, preserving the unmasked regions in their original form. This avoids re-encoding and decoding the intact audio, which—even with the high fidelity of \textit{WavTokenizer}—can introduce minor artifacts. To ensure smooth transitions between the original waveform and generated audio, we apply a short crossfade at the boundaries of each inpainted segment, linearly blending the waveforms over a 10 ms window. An overview of the inference pipeline is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:system_overview}.






\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:eval_methods}

To assess the performance of our audio inpainting framework, we adopt a combination of metrics for evaluations, capturing both the technical fidelity and perceptual plausibility of the reconstructed audio.


\subsection{Objective Metrics}
\label{subsec:metrics}

\paragraph{Fréchet Audio Distance (FAD).}  
We utilize the \textit{FAD} , an established perceptual metric that measures the distance between distributions of real and generated audio features~\cite{kilgour2018fr}. FAD assesses how similar the inpainted audio is to real music in terms of high-level perceptual attributes, such as timbre and texture, offering a robust indication of audio realism. In our evaluation we used VGG model.



\paragraph{Objective Difference Grade (ODG).}  
We evaluate perceptual audio quality using the ODG computed with the \textit{PEMO‑Q} model~\cite{huber2006pemo}. PEMO‑Q simulates the human auditory system to compare internal representations of the original and reconstructed signals. The resulting Perceptual Similarity Measure (PSM) is mapped to an ODG score ranging from 0 (imperceptible distortion) to –4 (very annoying artifacts). As a reference-based metric aligned with subjective listening tests, ODG complements waveform-based metrics by capturing perceptual fidelity.




\paragraph{Log Spectral Distance (LSD).}  
We also adopt the \textit{LSD}, following prior work such as~\cite{moliner2023diffusion}, to quantify spectral differences between original and reconstructed audio. LSD is defined as:

\begin{equation}
\text{LSD} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \sqrt{ \frac{1}{K} \sum_{k=1}^{K} \left( \log \left| X_{t,k} \right|^2 - \log \left| \hat{X}_{t,k} \right|^2 \right)^2 }
\end{equation}

where \(X_{t,k} = \mathrm{STFT}(x_0)\) and \(\hat{X}_{t,k} = \mathrm{STFT}(\hat{x}_0)\) are the STFT of the original and reconstructed signals, respectively. For our experiments, we use a window size of \(K = 2048\) samples and a hop length of 512 samples. This metric captures fine-grained spectral distortions introduced during inpainting.




\subsection{Experimental setup}
\label{subsec:Experimentalsetup}

We trained our model on the \textit{MusicNet} dataset~\cite{dataset}, which provides a predefined split of training and test sets. The training set comprises 330 freely-licensed classical music recordings.

During training, raw audio signals are first tokenized and truncated to a fixed length of 1024 tokens (approximately 13.6 seconds). A random timestep is selected, and noise (in the form of token masking) is applied to the corresponding segment. The DDM is then trained to reconstruct the original token sequence from this corrupted input. Through reverse diffusion steps, the model gradually denoises the token sequence, effectively learning to recover clean representations from noisy ones in token space, illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:training}.


