\begin{abstract}
%Time series foundation models have recently emerged as powerful tools capable of capturing complex temporal dependencies across various domains. In this study, we explore the capabilities of two prominent time series foundation models, Moirai and TimesFM, specifically addressing their effectiveness in solving the problem of flow prediction. Flow prediction involves forecasting future aggregated flows based on historical observations, a task with critical implications across transportation management, resource allocation, and urban planning. We systematically evaluate both Moirai and TimesFM against traditional forecasting methods using multiple real-world datasets. Our findings demonstrate that time series foundation models significantly outperform classical models, effectively capturing intricate temporal patterns inherent in flow data. This research not only highlights the practical applicability of Moirai and TimesFM as robust flow predictors but also establishes a promising direction for future development and application of foundation models in predictive analytics.

We investigate the effectiveness of time series foundation models (TSFMs) for crowd flow prediction, focusing on Moirai and TimesFM. Evaluated on three real-world mobility datasets—Bike NYC, Taxi Beijing, and Spanish national OD flows—these models are deployed in a strict zero-shot setting, using only the temporal evolution of each OD flow and no explicit spatial information. Moirai and TimesFM outperform both statistical and deep learning baselines, achieving up to 33\% lower RMSE, 39\% lower MAE and up to 49\% higher CPC compared to state-of-the-art competitors. Our results highlight the practical value of TSFMs for accurate, scalable flow prediction, even in scenarios with limited annotated data or missing spatial context.


\end{abstract}

