Table~\ref{tab:results} reports the performance of all evaluated models across three benchmark datasets. Results are presented in terms of RMSE, MAE, and CPC, providing a comprehensive picture of both prediction accuracy and the structural similarity between predicted and actual flows.

Classical statistical baselines (MA, ARIMA, VAR) deliver competitive but suboptimal results, while recent deep learning models (ST-ResNet, DMVSTNet, ACMF, CrowdNet, MSAGGN) achieve notably lower errors and higher CPC values, benefiting from architectures designed to capture spatial and temporal dependencies within the data explicitly.

Both TimeFM and Moirai (Large configuration), evaluated in a zero-shot setting, that is, without any fine-tuning or retraining on the target datasets. Both models consistently outperform all baselines and deep learning models across all three datasets. Moirai, in particular, achieves state-of-the-art results on every metric for each dataset (e.g., RMSE = 6.09, MAE = 3.04, CPC = 0.72 on Bike NYC; RMSE = 9.32, MAE = 7.34, CPC = 0.62 on Taxi BJ; RMSE = 21.74, MAE = 9.94, CPC = 0.67 on Spain OD). TimeFM follows closely. 

Overall, Moirai reduces the RMSE, CPC, and MAE by a -23.44\% 5.88\% -23.01\%  respectively in Bike NYC, -32.86\% 6.89\% -39.86\% in Taxi BJ, and -20.83\% 48.88\% -21.33\% using Spanish ODs with TimesFM achieving very similar results.

It is important to emphasize that TimeFM and Moirai accomplish this superior performance while operating under a stricter problem definition: they do not leverage explicit spatial relationships or dependencies among neighboring regions, as their input is limited to the temporal evolution of each origin-destination series independently. In contrast, deep learning baselines such as ST-ResNet, CrowdNet, and MSAGGN are specifically designed to model spatial interactions, which in theory should provide them with a notable advantage.

Despite this, the foundation models not only generalize successfully to new cities and data domains without retraining, but do so with less information.

In summary, these results underscore the versatility and generalization capabilities of time series foundation models for crowd flow prediction, even when trained in isolation from the spatial context that deep learning competitors exploit. This opens up promising perspectives for the application of foundation models in scenarios where both labeled data and fine-grained spatial information are limited or unavailable.

\section{Conclusions}
This work demonstrates the strong generalization and predictive capabilities of Moirai and TimesFM—in the challenging context of crowd flow prediction. Our zero-shot evaluation across diverse datasets reveals that these models not only outperform both classical and deep learning baselines in terms of RMSE, MAE, and CPC, but also achieve these results under more constrained input settings, with no access to explicit spatial relationships. The ability of foundation models to generalize across different spatial and temporal granularities and to operate effectively even when deprived of detailed spatial context, positions them as transformative tools for urban mobility analysis and beyond.

By reformulating the crowd flow prediction problem as a collection of per-origin time series forecasting tasks, we unlock the potential for scalable and flexible predictive modeling in real-world scenarios where data heterogeneity and annotation scarcity are common. Our findings open new avenues for research, suggesting that further exploration of foundation models—including their adaptation, fine-tuning, and extension to other domains—may yield substantial improvements for both predictive performance and practical deployment. In summary, time series foundation models mark a significant step forward in the evolution of flow prediction and human mobility analysis, offering a robust, generalizable, and data-efficient alternative to conventional modeling approaches.


