\subsection{Motif}
The field of Generative AI (GenAI) has bustled with recent developments in deep generative models for music and sound \cite{dhariwal2020jukeboxgenerativemodelmusic}, and even more importantly (and popularly) with large language models (LLMs) \cite{cao2023comprehensivesurveyaigeneratedcontent}. Much research has been conducted in attempts to utilize LLMs' remarkable abilities (by inducing reasoning through chains-of-thought in spatial contexts \cite{wei2023chainofthoughtpromptingelicitsreasoning}\cite{yamada2024l3golanguageagentschainof3dthoughts}, in image-splicing \cite{nath2025chatgptperformimagesplicing}, and even in crafty representations of financial assets \cite{sarkar}). It stands to reason that, with these unexpected applications arising at the intersection of disparate domains, it is worth exploring (and developing) cohesion between representations of an individual's taste in music and generative music models (like Udio or Suno\footnote{Our principal tool of use: \texttt{https://www.suno.com/home}}) through the \textcolor{red}{\emph{fabric of LLMs}}. 
\subsection{Recent \& Related Work}
\textbf{LLMs + Existing Deep Generative Models for Music}
\vspace{0.2cm}
\\ In an early study \cite{wei2025contextaitunescontextawareaigenerated}, Wei \emph{et al.} already explore developing \& integrating LLM-generated prompts to Suno in different environmental contexts tailored for stress relief benefits. The exact problem they pose (to the community) and investigate is: \quotebox{Does AI-generated music tailored to the user’s environment and stress level outperform pre-recorded relaxing music in effectiveness?}\vspace{0.15cm}
More generally, Ding \emph{et al.} explore composing lyrics and melodies using symbolic representations of songs as opposed to wavetransforms per usual. These representations themselves are entirely empirical in nature, making use of encodings with the $\log$-transform on a time series relative to its starting point. They employ LLMs primarily in the continuation of songs using these learned representations \cite{ding2025songcomposerlargelanguagemodel}.\\\\
\textbf{LLMs in detecting GenAI music}\vspace{0.2cm}
\\There is controversy and a general sense of alarm surrounding GenAI + Art (/music). To highlight and attempt to resolve this issue, in \cite{rahman2025sonicssyntheticidentifying}, methods are developed that reportedly excel at detecting ``counterfeit" music, generated through the means of Suno, and could presumably work for even more foundational models (like \cite{gong2025acestepstepmusicgeneration}).\newpage
\noindent{\textbf{Q\&A, Query, and Audio Datasets}}\vspace{0.2cm}
\\Tangential to our work and having the potential to cast a relatively unexplored space when combined with our work is the work of Deng\emph{ et al.} in \emph{MusiLingo}, specifically, the \verb|MI| dataset, and the question of if it can be utilised in improving generated prompts by improving the underlying LLM's reasoning capacity. \cite{deng2024musilingobridgingmusictext}. \\\\
There are a number of large, meticulously curated, and high-quality datasets used typically in ML + audio settings. These include the pure melody-based dataset \cite{raffel2016learning} (which can be worked with by conversion to MIDI), the recent LP-MusicCaps-MSD dataset primarily for song captioning \cite{doh2023lp}, and the single-note-based NPSynth dataset \cite{engel2017neural}. \\\\
\textbf{Abstractions and Representations}\vspace{0.2cm}
\\In relation to our work on formalising individuals' music preferences' representations, Anazbaghan \emph{et al.} probe LLMs to work purely on rudimentary textual representations with relative success \cite{anbazhagan2025probingaudiogenerationcapabilitiestextbased}. This serves as a natural baseline for our work to improve upon. \\\\
In \cite{sarkar}, \cite{hu2018deepstockrepresentationlearning}, representations of financial assets covered are particularly inspiring. There is a somewhat unintuitive parallel to be drawn here:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Candle stick CAE-image $\to$ 1D Audiowaveform's Spectrogram CAE-based image
    \item News Reports/Investor sentiments  $\to$ Music enthusiasts' review
    \item Company legal filings/press releases $\to$ individual's preferences
\end{itemize}
Also noteworthy is the prior discussion on the symbolic encoding method in \cite{ding2025songcomposerlargelanguagemodel}.
\subsection{Novelty Introduced}
TuneGenie introduces several key novel methods:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Novel Representation Method: }Motivated by the growing body of work on developing representations of financial assets using both ``more traditional" ML techniques \cite{hu2018deepstockrepresentationlearning} and LLMs \cite{sarkar}, we introduce a method to create an individual's representation in the context of the songs they prefer to listen to.
    \item \textbf{Cohesive Integration Method: }The traversal of extracting relevant data from playlists, etc., to LLM-generated prompts, and finally through the \verb|suno-api|, to music is non-trivial and unexplored. Admittedly, the work in \cite{wei2025contextaitunescontextawareaigenerated} comes close.
    \item \textbf{Robust Testing through Similarity Measures: }We test various similarity measures in pitting the generated music against the individual's preferred music to gauge the performance of our model. Song similarity has been explored in counterfeit-music detection, as in \cite{rahman2025sonicssyntheticidentifying}, but not in this ``noisy \& open-ended" context.
\end{enumerate}\newpage
\section{Methodology}
TuneGenie is implemented to replicate the seamlessness, low computational load, and ease in local execution of an end-to-end product. The computational and storage load is offset by reliance on Telegram's and Google's cloud services, which are available freely to all users. In a business setting, however, this would have to be endured by the producers. We incorporate an additional verifying agent into the prompt generator to add more accountability to our model.
\subsection{Model Architecture}
\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{architecture.png}
    \caption{Model Framework}
    \label{fig:enter-label}
\end{figure}
\noindent We now provide a brief description of each transition:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Telegram $\to$ Preference Data: To alleviate the computational resources expenses, at the moment, Telegram is used to facilitate going from a user's playlist, liked songs, followed artists, etc., to our data format.
    \item Preferred Data $\to^{*}$ LLM: The collated preferred data provided by the user is first converted into a representation of the user, which is supposed to improve the ease with which the LLM can handle this data.
    \item LLM $\to$ Forced Reasoning: This is a \emph{less rigorous} way of self-ask prompting \cite{press2023measuringnarrowingcompositionalitygap}, and is supposed to let the LLM internalise relevant inferences from representation level data. We pose a variety of questions, available in the appendix.
    \item Reasoned LLM State $\to$ Prompt Generation: Here, a \emph{verifying agent} is introduced, and detailed further in Figure 2. To contribute robustness to the prompts, we use the LLM's reasoning abilities to verify the correctness of these prompts given the user's preferences.
    \item Prompt $\to$ Final Song: Unfortunately, Suno AI \emph{does not offer} an API. To counter this issue, we used Selenium + ChromeDriver to automate the process of inputting the prompts. This is only possible in an academic setting. In the real world, a business license with Suno would likely need to be purchased.
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Representation Paradigm}
The dialogue surrounding a particular song or an artist carries crucial information about the sentiment that surrounds a set of songs. On the other hand, an individual's sentiment towards a set of songs or artists reflects some inherent characteristic. These are two sides of the same coin: matching music to man.  \\\\
The critical problem is that there is no structure to the various discourses on album releases on social media or press coverage of musical artists. There is also no structure to the primary sources that the industry collects for musical data—for instance, YouTube comments on an album. We create embeddings, which are multi-dimensional vectors of representation.  For each song and each user, there is a representative vector. For each song $s$, user $u$, the interaction:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf r_{s, u}=\overbrace{\Delta \boldsymbol{v}_{s} \cdot \boldsymbol{x}_{u}}^{\text {song contribution }}+\overbrace{\boldsymbol{v}_{s} \cdot \Delta \boldsymbol{x}_{u}}^{\text {user contribution }}+\varepsilon_{s, u}
\end{equation*}
Then, for user $u$ with $\deg(u)=k$, for example, the final pass $\mathbf{p}$ to the LLM is simply:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathbf{p}_u = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=0}^{k}\mathbf{r}_{i,u}
\end{equation*}
This is essentially a graphical approach to modelling the problem.
