\subsection{Recognizable morphisms}

In this subsection, we focus on some structural and combinatorial properties of morphisms that generate words admitting a unique factorization in circular factors, similarly to the notion of circular code \cite{codesautomata}. 

%An injective morphism $\morph : \Sigma^* \rightarrow \Gamma^*$ is \emph{circular} if for every $u,v\in \Gamma^*$, if $uv,vu\in \morph(\Sigma^*)$ then $u,v\in \morph(\Sigma^*)$.
%This definition comes from the definition of \emph{circular code}, also known as \emph{very-pure code}.


Let $\La\subseteq\Sigma^*$. An injective morphism $\morph:\Sigma^*\rightarrow\Gamma^*$ is \emph{recognizable} on $\morph(\La)$ if for every non-empty word $w\in\morph(\La)$ and every word $w'\in\R{w}$, there exist, and are unique, $p\in\Gamma^+$, $q\in\Gamma^*$, $z\in\Sigma^*$, and $c\in\Sigma$, such that $w' = q\morph(z)p$ and $pq=\morph(c)$.
If $\La=\Sigma^*$, we simply say that $\morph$ is recognizable.

% \begin{lemma}
% \label{le:circular=uniquecircfact}
%     A morphism $\morph:\Sigma^*\rightarrow\Gamma^*$ is circular if and only if, for every word $w\in\Sigma^+$ and every conjugate word $w'\in\R{w}$, there exist and are  unique  $p\in\Sigma^+$, $z,q\in\Sigma^*$, and $c\in\Sigma$, such that $\morph(w') = q\morph(z)p$ and $pq=\morph(c)$.
% \end{lemma}

In other words, every image under a recognizable morphism $\morph$ has a unique circular factorization in words of $\morph(\La)$. Equivalently, a recognizable morphism on $\La$ can be regarded as an injective map on the necklaces over $\La$, i.e., for all $x,y \in \La$, it holds that $\R{\morph(x)} = \R{\morph(y)}$ if and only if $\R{x}=\R{y}$.

