The following result shows an important structural property of the primitivity-preserving morphisms that are not recognizable. In particular, we prove that they can always be obtained by composing another morphism with the Thue--Morse morphism.

\begin{comment}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:thuemorse=rotation}
    Let $\morph : \{a,b\}^*\rightarrow\{a,b\}^*$ be a primitivity-preserving morphism. Then, $\morph(a)$ and $\morph(b)$ are rotations of one  another if and only if $\morph = \psi \circ \tmmorph$ for some morphism $\psi: \{a,b\}^*\rightarrow\{a,b\}^*$ and $\tmmorph$ is the Thue--Morse morphism. 
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    For the first direction, if $\morph$ is primitivity-preserving and $\morph(a)$ and $\morph(b)$ are rotations of one another, then there exist words $u,v\neq \varepsilon$ such that $\morph(a)=uv$ and $\morph(a)=vu$. We can then define $\psi=(u,v)$, and therefore $\morph=(\psi(\tau(a)),\psi(\tau(b))) = (\psi(ab),\psi(ba)) = (uv, vu)$.
    For the other direction, if $\morph=\psi\circ\tau$, then $\morph = (\psi(\tau(a)), \psi(\tau(b))) = (\psi(ab),\psi(ba)) = (\psi(a)\psi(b), \psi(b)\psi(a))$. Clearly, $\morph(a)$ and $\morph(b)$ are rotations of one another and the thesis follows.  
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
    Observe that when $\morph=\tmmorph$, the morphism $\psi$ in the previous proposition corresponds to the identity morphism $\psi=(a,b)$. 
\end{remark}

