\section{Characterization of binary BWT-run preserving morphisms}\label{sec:characterization_morphisms}


As a main result of this paper, we characterize the binary morphisms having a bounded BWT additive sensitivity function. In particular, we prove that they coincide with the primitivity-preserving morphisms. 

\begin{definition}
Let $k\geq 0$ be an integer. An acyclic morphism $\morph:\Sigma^*\rightarrow\Gamma^*$ is called \emph{$k$-BWT-run preserving} if for all $n\geq |\Sigma|$, $\bwas(n)\leq k$. We simply say \emph{BWT-run preserving} if such a $k$ exists.
\end{definition}

%In the next section, we focus on characterizing the class of binary morphisms $\morph$ with bounded function $\bwas$.
% Oppositely, a binary acyclic morphism cannot decrease the number of BWT runs of the word on which it is applied~\cite[Theorem~14]{Fici23}. Here we are interested in the combinatorial properties of morphisms that imply a constant upper bound on the additive and the multiplicative sensitivities. 


% Clearly, if the additive sensitivity is bounded, so is the multiplicative one. 

We first give a lemma, in which we prove that the finite-delay synchronization of a morphism on the images of a language results in a bounded increase in the number of BWT-runs.  The proof can be found in the Appendix \ref{ap:l32}. 
