Foundational models include SVR \cite{Drucker1997_SVR}, noted for handling non-linearities \cite{TayYao2001_SVRFinance,Kazem2013_SVRStock, PaiLin2005_HybridARIMASVM, OuLiu2010_SVRGARCHVol}, and tree-based ensembles like RF \cite{Breiman2001_RF} and XGBoost \cite{ChenGues2016_XGBoost}, valued for robustness and interaction modeling \cite{Talagala2018_AutoModelSelect}.

Deep learning (DL) advanced from ANNs/MLPs \cite{Rumelhart1986_BP, Zhang2003_ANNReviewTS} to RNNs \cite{Elman1990_RNN}. LSTMs \cite{Hochreiter1997_LSTM} and GRUs \cite{Cho2014_GRU} became key for sequential data due to their ability to learn long-term dependencies by addressing the vanishing gradient issue \cite{Bengio1994_VanishingGradient}. They are widely used in commodity forecasting \cite{Fischer2018_LSTMComm, Zhao2017_LSTMvsARIMAOil, Sermpinis2021_LSTMvsANNFinance, Yan2018_GRUCrudeOil, Chung2014_GRUvsLSTM}, with variants like bidirectional LSTMs \cite{Schuster1997_BiRNN} and CNN-LSTM hybrids \cite{Kim2016_CNNLSTMText,Livieris2020_CNNLSTMGold,LeCun1989_CNN,Bai2018_TCN}. The choice involves balancing complexity, data needs, and interpretability \cite{Hewamalage2021_FeatureEngLSTM}.

Transformer models \cite{Vaswani2017_Attention}, leveraging self-attention, represent a significant advancement for long-range dependency capture \cite{Lim2021_TransformerTSApps, Li2023_TransformerCrudeOil, Liu2022_TransformerAgri}. Recent architectures like PatchTST \cite{Nie2023_PatchTST} 
and FEDformer \cite{Zhou2022_FEDformer} 
highlight ongoing progress. Transformers are often integrated into hybrid models, frequently with decomposition methods \cite{Wu2021_VMDEMDTransformer}. Large Language Models (LLMs) are also influencing financial forecasting research \cite{Brown2020_GPT3,Wu2023_LLMFinance}.

Hybrid models are prevalent, often combining traditional methods with ML, or using a "decomposition and ensemble" strategy \cite{Zhang2003_HybridANNARIMA, Khashei2011_HybridARIMANN, Lahmiri2020_DecompEnsembleReview}. Decomposition techniques (e.g., Wavelets \cite{Daubechies1992_Wavelets}, VMD \cite{Dragomiretskiy2014_VMD}, EMD \cite{Huang1998_EMD}, SSA \cite{Golyandina2001_SSA}, STL \cite{Cleveland1990_STL}) simplify series for component-wise forecasting \cite{Aussem1999_WaveletNN, Hafezi2015_WaveletARIMATDNN, Hassani2009_SSANN, Li2018_VMDMLComm}. Integration of XAI is also an emerging trend \cite{Paul2021_AGRICAF}.

Performance is typically assessed using RMSE, MAE, and directional accuracy \cite{Hyndman2006_EvalMetrics}. Statistical tests like Diebold-Mariano \cite{DieboldMariano1995_DMTest} are sometimes used for comparing model accuracy. While ML/DL models often show superior performance, rankings are context-dependent \cite{Sun2018_LSTMCotton, Kara2011_SVMRaisin}.

\section{Methodology: The Regularized Sparse Autoencoder (RSAE) Framework}

This section details the Regularized Sparse Autoencoder (RSAE) framework, an approach specifically designed for the dual task of multi-horizon commodity futures price prediction and the discovery of interpretable latent market factors. The methodology draws inspiration from energy-based models \cite{LeCun2006_EBM} and sparse coding \cite{Olshausen1997_SparseCoding}, employing an explicit inference step to determine a sparse latent representation that best explains future price movements.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

We consider a set of $d$ market features at time $t$, $\mathbf{x}_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$. The model input is a look-back window $X = X_{t-W+1:t} = (\mathbf{x}_{t-W+1}, \ldots, \mathbf{x}_t) \in \mathbb{R}^{W \times d}$. Our objective is to predict the futures price $p_t$ at $N$ horizons $\tau_1 < \ldots < \tau_N$, with target $Y = Y_t = (p_{t+\tau_1}, \ldots, p_{t+\tau_N})^\top \in \mathbb{R}^N$. The RSAE learns a mapping from $X$ to $Y$ via a sparse latent variable $\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^m$ ($m \ll W \times d$).

\subsection{Model Architecture}

The RSAE comprises three neural network modules (Figure~\ref{fig:rsae_architecture}).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth,keepaspectratio]{RSAE.png}
\caption{Conceptual architecture of the Regularized Sparse Autoencoder (RSAE) framework.}
\label{fig:rsae_architecture}
\end{figure}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{History Predictor (Pred):} $\bm{h} = \text{Pred}(X; \theta_{\text{Pred}})$, maps $X$ to a context vector $\bm{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{h_{\text{dim}}}$, using LSTMs \cite{Hochreiter1997_LSTM}, GRUs \cite{Cho2014_GRU}, or 1D CNNs \cite{Bai2018_TCN}.
    \item \textbf{Decoder (Dec):} $\tilde{Y} = \text{Dec}(\bm{z}, \bm{h}; \theta_{\text{Dec}})$, predicts future prices $\tilde{Y} \in \mathbb{R}^N$ from $\bm{z}$ and $\bm{h}$.
    \item \textbf{Encoder (Enc) (for training):} $\hat{\bm{z}} = \text{Enc}(Y, \bm{h}; \theta_{\text{Enc}})$, performs amortized inference of $\bm{z}^*$ from actual future $Y$ and $\bm{h}$ \cite{Gershman2014_AmortizedInf,Kingma2013_VAE}. A separate encoder, $\text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X; \theta_{\text{XtoZ}})$, is trained to predict $\bm{z}^*$ from $X$ alone for test-time forecasting. $\text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}$ is trained with a mean-squared error objective to regress the K-step optimal z*; its parameters are not updated through the prediction loss.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Latent Variable Framework: Energy Function and Inference}

We define an energy function $E(Y, \bm{h}, \bm{z}) = C(Y, \text{Dec}(\bm{z}, \bm{h}; \theta_{\text{Dec}})) + \lambda R(\bm{z})$ \cite{LeCun2006_EBM}.
$C(Y, \tilde{Y}) = \frac{1}{N} \| Y - \tilde{Y} \|_2^2$ is the MSE prediction cost \cite{Bishop2006_PRML}.
$R(\bm{z}) = \|\bm{z}\|_1$ is the L1-norm sparsity regularizer \cite{Tibshirani1996_Lasso}.
The optimal latent variable $\bm{z}^* = \arg \min_{\bm{z}} E(Y, \bm{h}, \bm{z})$ is found via $K$-step (sub)gradient descent \cite{BoydVandenberghe2004_ConvexOpt}.

\subsubsection{Selection and Convergence of Inference Steps (K)}
\label{ssec:k_selection_convergence}
The number of iterative steps, $K$, in the inference process for $\bm{z}^*$ is a hyperparameter balancing thoroughness of energy minimization against computational cost. To determine an appropriate value for $K$, preliminary experiments were conducted on the validation set. This involved tracking the average energy function value, $E(Y, \bm{h}, \bm{z})$, over a range of potential iterations (e.g., $k=1$ to $k=15$) for multiple representative batches of validation samples.

The observed convergence behavior is depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:energy_convergence}. The mean energy (averaged across validation batches) typically exhibited a sharp decrease during the initial iterations (approximately $k=1$ to $k=7$). Beyond this initial phase, the rate of energy reduction substantially diminished, with the curve beginning to plateau around $k=8$ to $k=10$. While formal convergence to a global minimum is not guaranteed for non-convex energy landscapes, the iterative process aims to find a suitable local minimum that provides a sparse and predictive latent code. Based on the empirical observation that iterations beyond $K=10$ provided only marginal decreases in the objective energy at an increased computational cost, $K=10$ was selected for all subsequent experiments in this study. A more extensive analysis of convergence properties under varying conditions remains an avenue for future work.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{rsae_inference_convergence.png} 

\caption{Mean objective energy function value $E(Y, \bm{h}, \bm{z})$ versus the number of inference iterations ($k$), averaged over representative validation set batches. The plot demonstrates typical convergence behavior, with energy decreasing rapidly initially before plateauing. The selected $K=10$ (dashed red line) represents a point of diminishing returns for energy reduction.}
\label{fig:energy_convergence}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Test-Time Inference}
At test time, future $Y$ is unknown. We train an additional inference encoder, $\text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X; \theta_{\text{XtoZ}})$, supervised by the $\bm{z}^*$ values obtained during the training phase (from the $K$-step optimization using known $Y_{\text{train}}$). Thus, for a new test input $X_{\text{test}}$, the latent code is obtained directly: $\bm{z}_{\text{test}} = \text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X_{\text{test}}; \theta_{\text{XtoZ}})$. This amortized inference from $X$ alone is used for generating forecasts.

\subsection{Training Objective and Parameter Updates}
The primary training objective minimizes the prediction loss using the $K$-step inferred $\bm{z}^*$: $L_{\text{Pred}}^* = C(Y, \text{Dec}(\bm{z}^*, \bm{h}; \theta_{\text{Dec}}))$.
Parameters $\theta_{\text{Pred}}$ and $\theta_{\text{Dec}}$ are updated based on gradients from $L_{\text{Pred}}^*$. If the training-time Encoder $\text{Enc}(Y, \bm{h})$ is used, it is trained with an inference loss $L_{\text{Inf}} = \| \bm{z}^* - \text{Enc}(Y, \bm{h}) \|_2^2$. The test-time encoder $\text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X)$ is trained separately to predict $\bm{z}^*$ from $X$.

\subsection{Overall Training Algorithm}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initialize $\theta_{\text{Pred}}, \theta_{\text{Dec}}, \theta_{\text{XtoZ}}$.
    \item For each training batch $\{(X^{(j)}, Y^{(j)})\}$:
        \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\bm{h}^{(j)} = \text{Pred}(X^{(j)})$.
        \item Infer $\bm{z}^{*(j)}$ via $K$-step minimization of $E(Y^{(j)}, \bm{h}^{(j)}, \bm{z})$.
        \item $L_{\text{Pred}}^{*(j)} = C(Y^{(j)}, \text{Dec}(\bm{z}^{*(j)}, \bm{h}^{(j)}))$.
        \item Update $\theta_{\text{Pred}}, \theta_{\text{Dec}}$ via gradients of $\text{mean}(L_{\text{Pred}}^*)$ (e.g., Adam \cite{KingmaBa2014_Adam}).
        \item Update $\theta_{\text{XtoZ}}$ to predict $\bm{z}^{*(j)}$ from $X^{(j)}$.
        \end{enumerate}
    \item Validate using $L_{\text{Pred}}^*$ (with $\bm{z}_{\text{val}} = \text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X_{\text{val}})$) for tuning and early stopping \cite{Prechelt2012_EarlyStopping}.
    \item Test similarly. Analyze inferred $\bm{z}_{\text{test}}$ for interpretability.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Interpretability via Sparsity}
The L1 norm encourages sparse $\bm{z}^*$, aiding interpretability \cite{Tibshirani1996_Lasso}.

