Copper (LME nearby futures), Crude Oil (WTI, Brent front-month).
\subsubsection{Lagged Target Variables \& Price Dynamics (Daily)}
Historical prices, log returns, realized volatility \cite{AndersenBollerslev1998_RealizedVol}, futures curve spreads \cite{GortonRouwenhorst2006_CommodityFutures}.
\subsubsection{Macroeconomic Indicators (Daily, Monthly, Quarterly)}
Global/Major Economy PMIs, Industrial Production, inflation (CPI, PPI), interest/currency rates.
\subsubsection{Supply/Demand Fundamentals (Varying Frequencies)}
Copper: Exchange inventories, producer output, China consumption/imports, scrap indicators.
Crude Oil: EIA weekly inventories, US/OPEC+ production, rig counts, demand estimates.
\subsubsection{Market Sentiment \& Financial Data (Daily, Weekly)}
Futures volume/open interest, CFTC COT reports, VIX, OVX, S\&P 500, FTSE 100, Shanghai Comp. % Escaped S&P
\subsubsection{Technical Indicators (Calculated from Daily Prices)}
SMAs, EMAs, RSI, MACD, ADX \cite{Murphy1999_TechAnalysis}.
\subsubsection{Other (Exploratory)}
Inter-commodity spreads, weather indices (ENSO, hurricane impacts).

\subsection{Data Frequency and Time Period}
Primary modeling is daily. Lower frequency data forward-filled, with release lags respected for official stats (e.g., STEO) to prevent lookahead bias \cite{Ghysels2007_MIDAS}. Dataset: Jan 1, 2005 -- Dec 31, 2023.

\subsection{Data Pre-processing}
Standard steps \cite{Goodfellow2016_DeepLearning}: Missing data handling (remove if $\geq$40\%, else ffill), transformations for stationarity/skewness (log returns, log transform), Z-score normalization (params from training set only), cautious outlier treatment.

\subsection{Final Dataset Construction}
Inputs $X_t \in \mathbb{R}^{W \times d}$ ($W=60$), targets $Y_t \in \mathbb{R}^N$ ($N=3$ horizons: 1, 5, 22 days). Chronological splits: Train (2005--2018), Valid (2019--2020), Test (2021--2023) \cite{Bergmeir2012_TSCV}. Robustness could be improved with rolling-origin CV \cite{Bergmeir2018_TimeSeriesCVReview}. 

\section{Experimental Setup and Results}
The empirical results presented herein are based on the models trained and evaluated according to the setup detailed below.
\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\subsubsection*{RSAE Configuration}
Implemented using Python and JAX.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Input/Output:} $W=60$ days, $d \approx 50-70$ features. $Y_t = (\log(p_{t+1}), \log(p_{t+5}), \log(p_{t+22}))^\top$.
    \item \textbf{Architecture:} \texttt{Pred}: 2 stacked LSTMs (128 units each, dropout 0.2 \cite{Srivastava2014_Dropout}). \texttt{Dec}: MLP (hidden layers 64, 32 units; ReLU). A separate encoder $\text{Enc}_{\text{XtoZ}}(X)$ maps $X$ to $\bm{z}^*$ at test time. Latent dimension $m=16$.
    \item \textbf{Inference (Training):} $K=10$ gradient steps for $\bm{z}^*$, $\alpha_{\text{inf}} = 0.01$.
    \item \textbf{Sparsity:} $\lambda$ tuned via grid search ($\{10^{-5}, \dots, 10^{-2}\}$) on validation set; $\lambda = 5 \times 10^{-4}$ (Copper), $\lambda = 10^{-4}$ (Crude Oil).
    \item \textbf{Training:} Adam optimizer \cite{KingmaBa2014_Adam}, $\eta = 10^{-4}$, batch $B=64$, max 100 epochs, early stopping (patience 10 on $L_{\text{Pred}}^*$) \cite{Prechelt2012_EarlyStopping}.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Baseline Models}
Persistence, ARIMA \cite{BoxJenkins1970_ARIMA} (orders via `pmdarima` \cite{Akaike1974_AIC, Schwarz1978_BIC}), XGBoost \cite{ChenGues2016_XGBoost} (tuned via CV), Standard LSTM. Baseline hyperparameter tuning was standard; a fully balanced budget vs. RSAE is a future work consideration.


\subsubsection*{Evaluation Metrics}
Forecast performance was evaluated using standard metrics on log-transformed prices \cite{Hyndman2006_EvalMetrics}: Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE). For directional performance, we report several metrics:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Standard Directional Accuracy (DA):} The percentage of times the model correctly predicts the direction of price change from the current price $p_t$.
    \[
    DA_{\text{std}} = \frac{1}{T_{\text{test}}} \sum_{t \in \text{Test}} \mathbb{I}\left[\text{sign}(\hat{p}_{t+\tau_i} - p_t) = \text{sign}(p_{t+\tau_i} - p_t)\right]
    \]
    where $\text{sign}(0)$ is treated as 0. For this calculation, if both actual and predicted change are zero (within a small tolerance, $\epsilon_{\text{zero}}$), it is counted as a correct prediction.
    \item \textbf{DA Excluding No-Change (DA$_{\text{excl.zero}}$):} Standard DA calculated only on days where the actual price change $|p_{t+\tau_i} - p_t| > \epsilon_{\text{zero}}$. This mitigates the impact of periods with many flat price days.
    \item \textbf{Up-Move Hit Rate (UHR) \& Down-Move Hit Rate (DHR):} Calculated on the subset of days with actual price changes ($|p_{t+\tau_i} - p_t| > \epsilon_{\text{zero}}$). UHR is the proportion of actual up-moves correctly predicted as up-moves. DHR is the proportion of actual down-moves correctly predicted as down-moves.
    \item \textbf{Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC):} A robust measure for binary classification quality, considering true/false positives and negatives, calculated on the {up, down} predictions for days with actual price changes ($|p_{t+\tau_i} - p_t| > \epsilon_{\text{zero}}$). Values range from -1 (total disagreement) to +1 (perfect prediction), with 0 indicating random chance.
\end{itemize}
For RSAE, the average $L_0$ norm of $\bm{z}^*$ indicates sparsity. A threshold $\epsilon_{\text{zero}}$ (e.g., 0.05\% of average price or a fixed small value) was used to define "no-change" days for DA$_{\text{excl.zero}}$, UHR, DHR, and MCC.

