\textbf{Observations from Advanced Directional Metrics (for Copper, 1-day):}
Table~\ref{tab:advanced_da_metrics} suggests that when focusing on days with meaningful price movements (DA Excl. No-Change), the RSAE (59.53\%) maintains a slight edge over the LSTM (58.12\%). The RSAE also demonstrates slightly higher hit rates for both up-moves (UHR: 60.15\% vs. 58.90\%) and down-moves (DHR: 58.88\% vs. 57.31\%). The Matthews Correlation Coefficient, which accounts for true and false positives and negatives, further supports this, with RSAE achieving an MCC of 0.191 compared to LSTM's 0.163. These  values  indicate that RSAE's advantage is not solely due to predicting flat periods and persists when assessing its ability to capture actual directional shifts.

\textbf{Observations from Accuracy Results:}
RSAE generally achieves competitive or superior predictive performance. RMSE/MAE values are consistently lowest. DA is strong. Performance degrades with horizon. Average $L_0$ norm for $\bm{z}^*$ was $\approx 5.8$ (Copper) and $\approx 6.3$ (Crude Oil).

