\subsection{Interpretability Analysis of Latent Factors}
\label{sec:results_interpretability_main}
\subsubsection{Latent Factor Activity and Sparsity}
Typically 5-7 of 16 latent dimensions were active.
\subsubsection{Correlation with Input Features and Qualitative Economic Interpretation}
Interpretations are qualitative, based on correlations (Appendix~\ref{app:factor_correlations}) and event alignments. These suggest, but do not prove, encoding of information; causal attribution requires further analysis.

\textbf{Copper:} $z^*_{Cu,1}$ (Global Demand/Inv.), $z^*_{Cu,2}$ (Macro-Financial/USD), $z^*_{Cu,3}$ (Supply Shocks), $z^*_{Cu,4}$ (Sentiment/Flows).

\textbf{Crude Oil:} $z^*_{Oil,1}$ (US Demand/Inv.), $z^*_{Oil,2}$ (Geopolitics/OPEC+), $z^*_{Oil,3}$ (Risk Appetite), $z^*_{Oil,4}$ (Global ex-US Demand).



\subsection{Discussion of Results}
RSAE shows competitive accuracy while inducing sparse latent representations. Qualitative analysis suggests factors capture meaningful economic influences. L1 sparsity aids accuracy-interpretability. Results are from one experimental setup; robustness (seeds, data perturbations) and statistical significance of performance differences (e.g. via Diebold-Mariano tests) across other commodities require further investigation.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}

This study introduced an RSAE framework for multi-horizon commodity futures forecasting and interpretable factor discovery. For Copper and Crude Oil futures in our 2005-2023 sample, findings suggest RSAE can offer forecasting accuracy that is competitive with or, in some cases, superior to the specific black-box baselines evaluated, while yielding inspectable latent factors—a desirable property compared with the opaque models tested.

\subsection{Implications of Findings}
RSAE offers a path to accuracy with transparency \cite{Rudin2019_StopExplainBlackBox}, potentially enhancing decision support. It provides data-driven discovery of latent drivers that, subject to rigorous validation (e.g. quantitative factor mapping, expert labelling), might indicate regime shifts \cite{AngTimmermann2012_RegimeSwitch}. Validated sparse factors could improve risk model

\subsection{Limitations of the Study}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Interpretability validation is qualitative; quantitative methods are needed \cite{Lipton2018_MythosInterpretability}.
    \item Input feature dependence constrains learning.
    \item Model complexity implies higher computational cost/tuning effort.
    \item Adaptability to major structural breaks is a challenge \cite{Rossi2013_StructuralBreaks}.
    \item Sparsity level ($\lambda$) choice involves accuracy-interpretability trade-offs.
    \item Lacks intrinsic uncertainty quantification \cite{KendallGal2017_UncertaintyDL}.
    \item Experimental Design: Analysis uses a single chronological split; robust time-series CV (e.g., rolling-origin \cite{Bergmeir2018_TimeSeriesCVReview}) is needed. Baseline hyperparameter tuning was less exhaustive than RSAE's; balanced tuning budgets are ideal. The directional accuracy metrics presented (Standard DA, DA Excl. No-Change, UHR, DHR, MCC) offer more insight than simple DA, but a comprehensive analysis of these across all models and horizons, along with other metrics like the Pesaran-Timmermann test \cite{PesaranTimmermann1992_PTTest},
    is warranted. Formal statistical significance tests (Diebold-Mariano \cite{DieboldMariano1995_DMTest}, MCS \cite{Hansen2011_MCS})
    are essential for validating performance differences rigorously.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Avenues for Future Research}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Advanced Interpretability: SHAP \cite{LundbergLee2017_SHAP}, LIME \cite{Ribeiro2016_LIME}, NLP for factor labeling. SHAP on decoder for $\bm{z}^* \rightarrow \tilde{Y}$ contributions.
    \item Improved Inference: Variational methods \cite{Kingma2013_VAE,Rezende2014_StochasticBP}, adaptive optimization.
    \item Dynamic Sparsity Control: Learnable or context-varying $\lambda$.
    \item Regime-Switching Integration \cite{Hamilton1989_RegimeSwitch}.
    \item Uncertainty Quantification: Bayesian NNs \cite{Neal1995_BNN,MacKay1992_BayesNN}, MC dropout \cite{Gal2016_MCDropout}.
    \item Hierarchical/Cross-Asset Modeling.
    \item End-to-End Differentiable Optimization \cite{AmosKolter2017_OptNet, GregorLeCun2010_LISTA}.
    \item High-Frequency/Online Learning.
    \item Reproducibility: To enhance reproducibility and facilitate further research, efforts will be made to release appropriately anonymized preprocessed data, the modeling code (e.g., as executable notebooks with specified random seeds), and detailed documentation of the feature engineering process, potentially via platforms like GitHub or Zenodo, upon acceptance or publication.
    \item Advanced Benchmarking: Compare against PatchTST \cite{Nie2023_PatchTST}, FEDformer \cite{Zhou2022_FEDformer}; apply rigorous statistical tests (MCS \cite{Hansen2011_MCS}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Conclusion}
The RSAE framework, combining established deep learning techniques, shows potential for accurate, interpretable multi-horizon commodity futures forecasting. Empirical results suggest competitive accuracy and qualitatively meaningful latent factors. While acknowledging limitations (e.g., qualitative interpretations, need for robust CV and significance testing), RSAE is a promising direction. Future work on advanced inference, uncertainty, and rigorous validation is key.

