\subsection{Proxy Variable Construction (Illustrative)}
For the purpose of this study's interpretability analysis, two proxy variables were conceptually defined. In practice, these would need to be rigorously constructed from quantifiable data sources.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Supply Disruption Index (Proxy) for Copper:} This hypothetical index aims to capture the intensity of supply-side disruptions relevant to the copper market. It is conceptualized such that \textit{lower values of the index indicate more severe disruptions}. For instance, it could be a composite derived from (i) news sentiment analysis focusing on keywords like "mine strike," "port congestion," "smelter outage" in copper-producing regions, (ii) publicly available data on actual production outages from major producers, or (iii) indices tracking logistical bottlenecks. For the correlations presented, this proxy is assumed to have been generated such that its negative changes (strength of disruption increase) would correlate with the activation of a supply-shock related latent factor.
    \item \textbf{Geopolitical/OPEC+ Event (Proxy) for Crude Oil:} This hypothetical index aims to quantify the impact of geopolitical events or OPEC+ announcements that are perceived to tighten crude oil supply or increase risk premiums. \textit{Higher positive values of this index indicate events favoring tighter supply or higher risk}. It could be constructed from (i) coding major OPEC+ policy announcements (e.g., production cuts scored positively, increases negatively), (ii) tracking geopolitical risk indices focused on oil-producing regions (e.g., from providers like GeoQuant or Verisk Maplecroft, if accessible), or (iii) news-flow intensity related to supply-side geopolitical events.
\end{itemize}
The correlations below use these conceptual proxies.

